[2026-08-18 11:39:55] [agentic_pipeline.py (561)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1863312] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N7, N6\nN2 -> N1, N11\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N3, N10\nN4 -> N0, N12\nN5 -> N7, N2\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN11: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N4, N9\nN10 -> N5, N8\nN6 -> N1, N0\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN7: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N3, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Current node is N13. From N13, legal moves are to N3 or N10.  \n- Moving to N3: next player is at N3, which has edges to N4 and N9.  \n  - From N4: edges to N0 (dead end) and N12 (dead end).  \n  - From N9: edges to N7 and N6.  \n    - N7 and N6 both lead to dead ends (N7 has no outgoing edges, N6 → N1 and N0, both dead ends).  \n    - So from N9, opponent eventually loses.  \n- Moving to N10: next player is at N10, which has edges to N5 and N8.  \n  - N8 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N10 causes opponent to lose immediately. This is better than N3.  \nTherefore, choosing N10 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Current node is N13. From N13, legal moves are to N3 or N10.  \n- Moving to N3: next player is at N3, which has edges to N4 and N9.  \n  - From N4: edges to N0 (dead end) and N12 (dead end).  \n  - From N9: edges to N7 and N6.  \n    - N7 and N6 both lead to dead ends (N7 has no outgoing edges, N6 → N1 and N0, both dead ends).  \n    - So from N9, opponent eventually loses.  \n- Moving to N10: next player is at N10, which has edges to N5 and N8.  \n  - N8 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N10 causes opponent to lose immediately. This is better than N3.  \nTherefore, choosing N10 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N7, N6\nN2 -> N1, N11\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N3, N10\nN4 -> N0, N12\nN5 -> N7, N2\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN11: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N4, N9\nN10 -> N5, N8\nN6 -> N1, N0\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN7: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N3, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Current node is N13. From N13, legal moves are to N3 or N10.  \n- Moving to N3: leads to N4 or N9.  \n  - N4 → N0 or N12 (both terminal, opponent loses).  \n  - N9 → N7 or N6; N7 is terminal, N6 → N1 or N0 (both terminal).  \n  So from N3, opponent eventually reaches a terminal node.  \n- Moving to N10: leads to N5 or N8.  \n  - N8 is terminal (opponent loses immediately).  \n  - N5 → N7 or N2; N7 is terminal, N2 → N1 or N11 (both terminal).  \nSo both paths eventually end with opponent losing. However, moving to N10 gives an immediate win if opponent reaches N8. Since N8 is terminal and opponent loses immediately upon reaching it, choosing N10 is better because it provides a direct path to an opponent loss. Thus, N10 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Current node is N13. From N13, legal moves are to N3 or N10.  \n- Moving to N3: leads to N4 or N9.  \n  - N4 → N0 or N12 (both terminal, opponent loses).  \n  - N9 → N7 or N6; N7 is terminal, N6 → N1 or N0 (both terminal).  \n  So from N3, opponent eventually reaches a terminal node.  \n- Moving to N10: leads to N5 or N8.  \n  - N8 is terminal (opponent loses immediately).  \n  - N5 → N7 or N2; N7 is terminal, N2 → N1 or N11 (both terminal).  \nSo both paths eventually end with opponent losing. However, moving to N10 gives an immediate win if opponent reaches N8. Since N8 is terminal and opponent loses immediately upon reaching it, choosing N10 is better because it provides a direct path to an opponent loss. Thus, N10 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N7, N6\nN2 -> N1, N11\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N3, N10\nN4 -> N0, N12\nN5 -> N7, N2\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN11: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N4, N9\nN10 -> N5, N8\nN6 -> N1, N0\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN7: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N3, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Current node is N13. From N13, legal moves are to N3 or N10.  \n- Moving to N3: next node is N4 or N9.  \n  - N4 → N0 or N12 (both terminal, opponent loses).  \n  - N9 → N7 or N6; N7 is terminal, N6 → N1 or N0 (both terminal).  \n  So from N3, opponent eventually faces a terminal node.  \n- Moving to N10: next node is N5 or N8.  \n  - N8 is terminal (opponent loses immediately).  \n  - N5 → N7 or N2; N7 is terminal, N2 → N1 or N11 (both terminal).  \nSo both moves eventually lead to opponent losing.  \nBut from N10, if we go to N8, opponent loses immediately. That is a winning move.  \nThus, choosing N10 leads to an immediate loss for the opponent.  \nTherefore, N10 is better than N3.  \nFinal choice: N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Current node is N13. From N13, legal moves are to N3 or N10.  \n- Moving to N3: next node is N4 or N9.  \n  - N4 → N0 or N12 (both terminal, opponent loses).  \n  - N9 → N7 or N6; N7 is terminal, N6 → N1 or N0 (both terminal).  \n  So from N3, opponent eventually faces a terminal node.  \n- Moving to N10: next node is N5 or N8.  \n  - N8 is terminal (opponent loses immediately).  \n  - N5 → N7 or N2; N7 is terminal, N2 → N1 or N11 (both terminal).  \nSo both moves eventually lead to opponent losing.  \nBut from N10, if we go to N8, opponent loses immediately. That is a winning move.  \nThus, choosing N10 leads to an immediate loss for the opponent.  \nTherefore, N10 is better than N3.  \nFinal choice: N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N7, N6\nN2 -> N1, N11\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N3, N10\nN4 -> N0, N12\nN5 -> N7, N2\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN11: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N4, N9\nN10 -> N5, N8\nN6 -> N1, N0\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN7: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N3, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Current node is N13. From N13, legal moves are to N3 or N10.  \n- Moving to N3: leads to N4 or N9.  \n  - N4 → N0 or N12 (both terminal, opponent loses).  \n  - N9 → N7 or N6.  \n    - N7 is terminal (opponent loses).  \n    - N6 → N1 or N0 (both terminal).  \n  So from N3, opponent eventually faces a terminal node.  \n- Moving to N10: leads to N5 or N8.  \n  - N8 is terminal (opponent loses immediately).  \n  - N5 → N7 or N2.  \n    - N7 is terminal.  \n    - N2 → N1 or N11 (both terminal).  \nSo both N3 and N10 lead to opponent losing eventually. However, N10 gives an immediate win (N8 is terminal), so opponent loses immediately upon reaching N8. Thus, moving to N10 is better because it ends the game immediately. Therefore, choosing N10 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Current node is N13. From N13, legal moves are to N3 or N10.  \n- Moving to N3: leads to N4 or N9.  \n  - N4 → N0 or N12 (both terminal, opponent loses).  \n  - N9 → N7 or N6.  \n    - N7 is terminal (opponent loses).  \n    - N6 → N1 or N0 (both terminal).  \n  So from N3, opponent eventually faces a terminal node.  \n- Moving to N10: leads to N5 or N8.  \n  - N8 is terminal (opponent loses immediately).  \n  - N5 → N7 or N2.  \n    - N7 is terminal.  \n    - N2 → N1 or N11 (both terminal).  \nSo both N3 and N10 lead to opponent losing eventually. However, N10 gives an immediate win (N8 is terminal), so opponent loses immediately upon reaching N8. Thus, moving to N10 is better because i