here opponent can eventually force a win.  \n- Moving to N10: next player is at N10, which has edges to N5 and N8.  \n  - N8 is a dead end (opponent loses immediately).  \n  - N5 leads to N7 and N2.  \n    - N7 is dead end.  \n    - N2 leads to N1 and N11, both dead ends.  \n  - So from N10, opponent can move to N8 and win immediately.  \nThus, moving to N10 allows the opponent to win immediately via N8.  \nMoving to N3 gives opponent a path but not immediate win. However, from N3 → N4 → N0 or N12 (dead ends), or N9 → N7 or N6 → N1 or N0 (dead ends). All paths eventually end.  \nBut the key is: if you move to a node with no outgoing edges, opponent loses.  \nN10 has outgoing edges, so opponent can move.  \nN3 has outgoing edges.  \nBut from N10, opponent can go to N8 (dead end) → you lose.  \nSo if you go to N10, opponent goes to N8 and wins.  \nTherefore, N10 is bad.  \nGo to N3. From N3, opponent goes to N4 or N9.  \n- N4 → N0 or N12 (dead ends) → you win.  \n- N9 → N7 or N6 → both lead to dead ends.  \nSo opponent is forced into a dead end eventually.  \nThus, N3 is a winning move.  \nTherefore, best move is N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Current node is N13. From N13, legal moves are to N3 or N10.  \n- Moving to N3: next player is at N3, which has edges to N4 and N9.  \n  - From N4: edges to N0 (dead end) and N12 (dead end).  \n  - From N9: edges to N7 and N6.  \n    - N7 and N6 both lead to N1 or N0, which are dead ends.  \n  - So N3 leads to a position where opponent can eventually force a win.  \n- Moving to N10: next player is at N10, which has edges to N5 and N8.  \n  - N8 is a dead end (opponent loses immediately).  \n  - N5 leads to N7 and N2.  \n    - N7 is dead end.  \n    - N2 leads to N1 and N11, both dead ends.  \n  - So from N10, opponent can move to N8 and win immediately.  \nThus, moving to N10 allows the opponent to win immediately via N8.  \nMoving to N3 gives opponent a path but not immediate win. However, from N3 → N4 → N0 or N12 (dead ends), or N9 → N7 or N6 → N1 or N0 (dead ends). All paths eventually end.  \nBut the key is: if you move to a node with no outgoing edges, opponent loses.  \nN10 has outgoing edges, so opponent can move.  \nN3 has outgoing edges.  \nBut from N10, opponent can go to N8 (dead end) → you lose.  \nSo if you go to N10, opponent goes to N8 and wins.  \nTherefore, N10 is bad.  \nGo to N3. From N3, opponent goes to N4 or N9.  \n- N4 → N0 or N12 (dead ends) → you win.  \n- N9 → N7 or N6 → both lead to dead ends.  \nSo opponent is forced into a dead end eventually.  \nThus, N3 is a winning move.  \nTherefore, best move is N3.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N12, N13, N10\nN0 -> N7, N9\nN2 -> N1, N12\nN13 -> N6\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN11 -> N4\nN10: no outgoing edges\nN7 -> N8, N2, N13\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N8, N11, N0\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N5\nN6: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N8, N11, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Current node is N3. From N3, legal moves are to N8, N11, or N0.  \n- Moving to N8: leads to N5, which has edges to N12, N13, N10 (all terminal). The opponent then moves from N5 to one of these terminals, and then you lose because the opponent reaches a terminal node.  \n- Moving to N11: leads to N4, which has no outgoing edges. The opponent cannot move and loses. This is a winning move.  \n- Moving to N0: leads to N7, N9 (both have no outgoing edges or lead to dead ends). From N7, opponent can go to N8, N2, N13 — N2 leads to N1 (terminal), N13 to N6 (terminal), N8 to N5 (which branches to terminals). The opponent can then force a win.  \n\nThus, moving to N11 immediately ends the game with opponent losing. This is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Current node is N3. From N3, legal moves are to N8, N11, or N0.  \n- Moving to N8: leads to N5, which has edges to N12, N13, N10 (all terminal). The opponent then moves from N5 to one of these terminals, and then you lose because the opponent reaches a terminal node.  \n- Moving to N11: leads to N4, which has no outgoing edges. The opponent cannot move and loses. This is a winning move.  \n- Moving to N0: leads to N7, N9 (both have no outgoing edges or lead to dead ends). From N7, opponent can go to N8, N2, N13 — N2 leads to N1 (terminal), N13 to N6 (terminal), N8 to N5 (which branches to terminals). The opponent can then force a win.  \n\nThus, moving to N11 immediately ends the game with opponent losing. This is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N11</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N12, N13, N10\nN0 -> N7, N9\nN2 -> N1, N12\nN13 -> N6\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN11 -> N4\nN10: no outgoing edges\