\n\n- N4 → N3 → N11 → N1 (terminal, opponent loses after N1, which has no outgoing edges). After N4, opponent moves to N3. From N3, opponent can go to N11, N13, or N7. If opponent chooses N11 → N1 (terminal), then we lose. If opponent chooses N13 → N6 → N8 (terminal), or N6 → N9 (terminal), or N6 → N11 → N1 (terminal). All paths eventually end with opponent losing. So from N4, opponent can force a win or draw depending on choice, but not clearly a loss.\n\n- N3 → N11 → N1 (terminal), or N13 → N6 → N8/N9/N11 → all end with opponent losing. From N3, opponent can move to N7 (terminal), so if opponent goes to N7, they lose. So opponent will avoid N7 and go to N11 or N13. From N11 → N1 (terminal), so after N11, opponent loses. So from N3, opponent moves to N11 → we lose. So if we go to N3, opponent wins.\n\n- N5 → N0 (terminal), so opponent loses immediately. So if we move to N5, opponent has no move and loses.\n\nThus, moving to N5 results in immediate opponent loss. This is optimal.\n\nTherefore, best move is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N12, legal moves are to N4, N3, or N5. We analyze each:\n\n- N4 → N3 → N11 → N1 (terminal, opponent loses after N1, which has no outgoing edges). After N4, opponent moves to N3. From N3, opponent can go to N11, N13, or N7. If opponent chooses N11 → N1 (terminal), then we lose. If opponent chooses N13 → N6 → N8 (terminal), or N6 → N9 (terminal), or N6 → N11 → N1 (terminal). All paths eventually end with opponent losing. So from N4, opponent can force a win or draw depending on choice, but not clearly a loss.\n\n- N3 → N11 → N1 (terminal), or N13 → N6 → N8/N9/N11 → all end with opponent losing. From N3, opponent can move to N7 (terminal), so if opponent goes to N7, they lose. So opponent will avoid N7 and go to N11 or N13. From N11 → N1 (terminal), so after N11, opponent loses. So from N3, opponent moves to N11 → we lose. So if we go to N3, opponent wins.\n\n- N5 → N0 (terminal), so opponent loses immediately. So if we move to N5, opponent has no move and loses.\n\nThus, moving to N5 results in immediate opponent loss. This is optimal.\n\nTherefore, best move is N5.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N4\n\nDirected graph:\nN4 -> N9, N1\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN5 -> N0, N3\nN3 -> N7\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN9 -> N3, N2\nN7: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N5, N6\nN1 -> N8, N0\nN6: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N9, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N9 or N1.  \n- Moving to N9 leads to N3 or N2.  \n  - N3 leads to N7 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n  - N2 leads to N5 or N6; N5 leads to N0 (terminal) or N3 (terminal); N6 is terminal.  \n  So from N9, opponent can be forced into terminal states.  \n- Moving to N1 leads to N8 or N0, both terminal. Opponent immediately loses.  \n\nThus, moving to N1 immediately ends the game with opponent losing. This is a winning move.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N4, the legal moves are to N9 or N1.  \n- Moving to N9 leads to N3 or N2.  \n  - N3 leads to N7 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n  - N2 leads to N5 or N6; N5 leads to N0 (terminal) or N3 (terminal); N6 is terminal.  \n  So from N9, opponent can be forced into terminal states.  \n- Moving to N1 leads to N8 or N0, both terminal. Opponent immediately 