Thus, moving to N13 wins immediately.  \nTherefore, choosing N13 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Current node is N4. From N4, legal moves are to N9 or N13.  \n- Moving to N9: next player faces N9, which has edges to N10 and N5.  \n  - From N10: edges to N8 (loss) or N6.  \n    - N6 leads to N12 or N11 (both end in no outgoing edges or loops).  \n    - N12 → N1, N2 (N2 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses).  \n    - So from N10, opponent can go to N6 → N12 → N1 → end (opponent loses).  \n  - From N5: edges to N8 (loss) or N0.  \n    - N0 → N3 (loss) or N1 (loss).  \n    - So from N5, opponent loses immediately.  \n  Thus, from N9, opponent can only go to N10 or N5, both of which eventually lead to a losing position for the opponent.  \n- Moving to N13: next player faces N13 → N7 or N11.  \n  - N7 and N11 have no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N13 wins immediately.  \nTherefore, choosing N13 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N13</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN5 -> N4, N10, N1, N7\nN11: no outgoing edges\nN4 -> N3, N6\nN10 -> N11\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N9, N3, N2\nN9 -> N0\nN2 -> N8\nN3 -> N12\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N4, N5, N7\nN7: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N9, N3, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reaso