l terminal. \n  - N5 → N0 → N4/N6 (terminal). \n  - N1 → terminal. \n  So opponent has options, but all lead to terminal nodes. Since opponent moves after us, they will eventually lose. Still good.\n\n- Move to N10: leads to N11 (terminal). Opponent loses immediately. This is a win.\n\nThus, moving to N10 results in immediate loss for opponent. Best outcome.\n\nAnswer: N10</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, legal moves are to N5, N7, or N10. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N5: leads to N0 or N9. N9 is terminal (opponent loses). N0 leads to N4 or N6, both terminal. So opponent eventually loses. Good.\n\n- Move to N7: leads to N12. From N12, opponent can go to N5, N13, or N1. \n  - N13 → N3 → N6 (terminal), or N4 (terminal), or N2 (terminal). All terminal. \n  - N5 → N0 → N4/N6 (terminal). \n  - N1 → terminal. \n  So opponent has options, but all lead to terminal nodes. Since opponent moves after us, they will eventually lose. Still good.\n\n- Move to N10: leads to N11 (terminal). Opponent loses immediately. This is a win.\n\nThus, moving to N10 results in immediate loss for opponent. Best outcome.\n\nAnswer: N10</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N0, N9\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN12 -> N5, N13, N1\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN10 -> N11\nN7 -> N12\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N3, N4, N2\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N5, N7, N10\nN0 -> N4, N6\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N6\nN11: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N5, N7, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, legal moves are to N5, N7, or N10. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N5: leads to N0 or N9. N9 is terminal (opponent loses), N0 leads to N4 or N6 (both terminal). So opponent loses immediately if we go to N9. But from N0, opponent can go to N4 or N6 (both terminal), so opponent loses after our move. Thus, N5 leads to opponent losing soon.\n\n- Move to N7: leads to N12. From N12, opponent can go to N5, N13, or N1. N1 is terminal (opponent loses). N5 → N0 or N9 (both terminal). N13 → N3, N4, N2 (all terminal). So opponent has options, but all lead to terminal nodes eventually. Since opponent moves from N12, they can choose N1 and win immediately. So N7 allows opponent to win.\n\n- Move to N10: leads to N11 (terminal). Opponent has no moves, so opponent loses. This is a winning move.\n\nThus, moving to N10 forces the opponent into a terminal node immediately. This is the best outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, legal moves are to N5, N7, or N10. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N5: leads to N0 or N9. N9 is terminal (opponent loses), N0 leads to N4 or N6 (both terminal). So opponent loses immediately if we go to N9. But from N0, opponent can go to N4 or N6 (both terminal), so opponent loses after our move. Thus, N5 leads to opponent losing soon.\n\n- Move to N7: leads to N12. From N12, opponent can go to N5, N13, or N1. N1 is terminal (opponent loses). N5 → N0 or N9 (both terminal). N13 → N3, N4, N2 (all terminal). So opponent has options, but all lead to terminal nodes eventually. Since opponent moves from N12, they can choose N1 and win immediately. So N7 allows opponent to win.\n\n- Move to N10: leads to N11 (terminal). Opponent has no moves, so opponent loses. This is a winning move.\n\nThus, moving to N10 forces the opponent into a terminal node immediately. This is the best outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N0, N9\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN12 -> N5, N13, N1\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN10 -> N11\nN7 -> N12\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N3, N4, N2\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N5, N7, N10\nN0 -> N4, N6\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N6\nN11: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N5, N7, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, legal moves are to N5, N7, or N10. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N5: leads to N0 or N9. N9 is terminal (opponent loses). N0 leads to N4 or N6, both terminal. So opponent loses after N0 → N4 or N6. Thus, N5 forces opponent into a losing position.\n- Move to N7: leads to N12. From N12, opponent can go to N5, N13, or N1. N1 is terminal. N13 leads to N3, N4, N2 — all terminal. N5 leads to N0 or N9 (terminal). So all options from N12 eventually end with opponent losing. So N7 also forces opponent into a losing position.\n- Move to N10: leads to N11, which is terminal — opponent loses immediately.\n\nAll three moves result in opponent losing. However, in N5, N7, and N10, the opponent is forced into a terminal node or a chain ending in a terminal node. Since the goal is to maximize outcome and all moves win, we pick one that is optimal. Since N10 leads directly to N11 (immediate terminal), it is the most direct and secure win. Thus, N10 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, legal moves are to N5, N7, or N10. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N5: leads to N0 or N9. N9 is terminal (opponent loses). N0 leads to N4 or N6, both terminal. So opponent loses after N0 → N4 or N6. Thus, N5 forces opponent into a losing position.\n- Move to N7: leads to N12. From N12, opponent can go to N5, N13, or N1. N1 is terminal. N13 leads to N3, N4, N2 — all terminal. N5 leads to N0 or N9 (terminal). So all options from N12 eventually end with opponent losing. So N7 also forces opponent into a losing position.\n- Move to N10: leads to N11, which is terminal — opponent loses immediately.\n\nAll three moves result in opponent losing. However, in N5, N7, and N10, the opponent is forced into a terminal node or a chain ending in a terminal node. Since the goal is to maximize outcome and all moves win, we pick one that is optimal. Since N10 leads directly to N11 (immediate terminal), it is the most direct and secure win. Thus, N10 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N0, N9\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN12 -> N5, N13, N1\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN10 -> N11\nN7 -> N12\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N3, N4, N2\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N5, N7, N10\nN0 -> N4, N6\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N6\nN11: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N5, N7, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, legal moves are to N5, N7, or N10. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N5: leads to N0 or N9. N9 is a terminal (no outgoing edges), so opponent loses immediately if they are at N9. But from N0, opponent can go to N4 or N6 — both terminal. So opponent loses on their turn from N0 or N6. Thus, from N5, opponent is forced into a terminal node on their move — we win.\n\n- Move to N7: leads to N12. From N12, opponent can go to N5, N13, or N1. N1 is terminal. N5 leads to N0 or N9 (both terminal). N13 leads to N3, N4, N2 — all eventually terminal. So opponent can avoid immediate loss, but all paths lead to terminal nodes eventually. However, since opponent moves next, and every path leads to terminal nodes after their move, they will eventually lose. So this is a winning move.\n\n- Move to N10: leads to N11 — terminal. Opponent has no move, so they lose immediately.\n\nThus, moving to N10 wins immediately. It is the simplest and most direct winning move.\n\nTherefore, best choice is N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, legal moves are to N5, N7, or N10. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N5: leads to N0 or N9. N9 is a terminal (no outgoing edges), so opponent loses immediately if they are at N9. But from N0, opponent can go to N4 or N6 — both terminal. So opponent loses on their turn from N0 or N6. Thus, from N5, opponent is forced into a terminal node on their move — we win.\n\n- Move to N7: leads to N12. From N12, opponent can go to N5, N13, or N1. N1 is terminal. N5 leads to N0 or N9 (both terminal). N13 leads to N3, N4, N2 — all eventually terminal. So opponent can avoid immediate loss, but all paths lead to terminal nodes eventually. However, since opponent moves next, and every path leads to terminal nodes after their move, they will eventually lose. So this is a winning move.\n\n- Move to N10: leads to N11 — terminal. Opponent has no move, so they lose immediately.\n\nThus, moving to N10 wins immediately. It is the simplest and most direct winning move.\n\nTherefore, best choice is N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N0, N9\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN12 -> N5, N13, N1\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN10 -> N11\nN7 -> N12\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N3, N4, N2\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N5, N7, N10\nN0 -> N4, N6\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N6\nN11: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N5, N7, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, legal moves are to N5, N7, or N10. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N5: leads to N0 or N9. N9 is terminal (opponent loses). N0 leads to N4 or N6, both terminal. So opponent loses immediately after our move to N5 → N9 or N0 → terminal. So opponent loses on next turn → we win.\n\n- Move to N7: leads to N12. From N12, opponent can go to N5, N13, or N1. \n  - N13 → N3 → N6 (terminal), or N4 (terminal), or N2 (terminal). All terminal. \n  - N5 → N0 → N4/N6 (terminal). \n  - N1 → terminal. \n  So opponent can force a win from N12? No — all moves from N12 lead to terminal nodes eventually. So opponent loses after their turn. Thus, we win.\n\n- Move to N10: leads to N11 (terminal). Opponent has no move → opponent loses immediately.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing. But we want to maximize outcome. Since all result in win, we pick one. N10 leads to immediate terminal (opponent has no move), which is optimal. So best is N10.\n\n</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, legal moves are to N5, N7, or N10. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N5: leads to N0 or N9. N9 is terminal (opponent loses). N0 leads to N4 or N6, both terminal. So opponent loses immediately after our move to N5 → N9 or N0 → terminal. So opponent loses on next turn → we win.\n\n- Move to N7: leads to N12. From N12, opponent can go to N5, N13, or N1. \n  - N13 → N3 → N6 (terminal), or N4 (terminal), or N2 (terminal). All terminal. \n  - N5 → N0 → N4/N6 (terminal). \n  - N1 → terminal. \n  So opponent can force a win from N12? No — all moves from N12 lead to terminal nodes eventually. So opponent loses after their turn. Thus, we win.\n\n- Move to N10: leads to N11 (terminal). Opponent has no move → opponent loses immediately.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing. But we want to maximize outcome. Since all result in win, we pick one. N10 leads to immediate terminal (opponent has no move), which is optimal. So best is N10.\n\n</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N0, N9\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN12 -> N5, N13, N1\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN10 -> N11\nN7 -> N12\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N3, N4, N2\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N5, N7, N10\nN0 -> N4, N6\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N6\nN11: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N5, N7, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N8, legal moves are to N5, N7, or N10. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N5: N5 → N0 or N9. N9 has no outgoing edges, so opponent loses immediately if they move from N9. But from N0, opponent can go to N4 (dead end) or N6 (dead end). So opponent has no moves from N0 or N9. Thus, if we go to N5, opponent is forced into a dead end. So this is a winning move.\n\n- Move to N7: N7 → N12 → N5, N13, N1. N1 is dead end, N5 leads to N0/N9 (dead ends), N13 → N3 → N6 (dead end), or N4 (dead end), N2 (dead end). All paths lead to dead ends. So opponent will eventually lose. This is also a winning move.\n\n- Move to N10: N10 → N11 (dead end). Opponent loses immediately. This is also a winning move.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing immediately or in one step. But we want to maximize outcome. Since all lead to opponent losing, any is valid. However, choosing N10 leads to immediate loss for opponent (no moves from N11). This is simplest and fastest win. So best choice is N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N8, legal moves are to N5, N7, or N10. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N5: N5 → N0 or N9. N9 has no outgoing edges, so opponent loses immediately if they move from N9. But from N0, opponent can go to N4 (dead end) or N6 (dead end). So opponent has no moves from N0 or N9. Thus, if we go to N5, opponent is forced into a dead end. So this is a winning move.\n\n- Move to N7: N7 → N12 → N5, N13, N1. N1 is dead end, N5 leads to N0/N9 (dead ends), N13 → N3 → N6 (dead end), or N4 (dead end), N2 (dead end). All paths lead to dead ends. So opponent will eventually lose. This is also a winning move.\n\n- Move to N10: N10 → N11 (dead end). Opponent loses immediately. This is also a winning move.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing immediately or in one step. But we want to maximize outcome. Since all lead to opponent losing, any is valid. However, choosing N10 leads to immediate loss for opponent (no moves from N11). This is simplest and fastest win. So best choice is N10.</reason>\n<answer>N10</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN7 -> N5, N0\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N4, N1, N7\nN4 -> N9\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N4, N2, N3\nN0 -> N2\nN1 -> N8\nN9: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N4, N1, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, legal moves are to N4, N1, or N7.  \n- Moving to N4: goes to N4 → N9 (terminal), opponent loses immediately. So I win.  \n- Moving to N1: goes to N1 → N8 → N4 → N9 (terminal), opponent eventually loses. I win.  \n- Moving to N7: goes to N7 → N5 (terminal) or N7 → N0 → N2 (terminal). Either way, opponent loses. I win.  \n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing. But we want to maximize outcome — any move wins. Choose one that leads to a decisive win. N4 leads directly to terminal node N9. Simple and secure. Thus, N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, legal moves are to N4, N1, or N7.  \n- Moving to N4: goes to N4 → N9 (terminal), opponent loses immediately. So I win.  \n- Moving to N1: goes to N1 → N8 → N4 → N9 (terminal), opponent eventually loses. I win.  \n- Moving to N7: goes to N7 → N5 (terminal) or N7 → N0 → N2 (terminal). Either way, opponent loses. I win.  \n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing. But we want to maximize outcome — any move wins. Choose one that leads to a decisive win. N4 leads directly to terminal node N9. Simple and secure. Thus, N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN7 -> N5, N0\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N4, N1, N7\nN4 -> N9\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N4, N2, N3\nN0 -> N2\nN1 -> N8\nN9: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N4, N1, N7assistant\n", "response": "<reason>From N6, legal moves are to N4, N1, or N7.  \n- N4 → N9 (N9 has no outgoing edges, so next player loses → current player wins).  \n- N1 → N8 → N4 → N9 (N9 has no outgoing edges, opponent loses after their move → current player wins).  \n- N7 → N5 (N5 has no outgoing edges, opponent loses → current player wins).  \n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing immediately. However, we must choose the one that maximizes outcome. Since all result in win, we pick the one that gives most control or avoids risk. N7 leads directly to N5 (opponent loses immediately). This is a clean, direct win. N4 and N1 lead to longer chains but still win. N7 is simplest and most direct. Thus, N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>From N6, legal moves are to N4, N1, or N7.  \n- N4 → N9 (N9 has no outgoing edges, so next player loses → current player wins).  \n- N1 → N8 → N4 → N9 (N9 has no outgoing edges, opponent loses after their move → current player wins).  \n- N7 → N5 (N5 has no outgoing edges, opponent loses → current player wins).  \n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing immediately. However, we must choose the one that maximizes outcome. Since all result in win, we pick the one that gives most control or avoids risk. N7 leads directly to N5 (opponent loses immediately). This is a clean, direct win. N4 and N1 lead to longer chains but still win. N7 is simplest and most direct. Thus, N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}]
