e moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N0\nN11 -> N4, N9\nN7: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N8, N7\nN6 -> N13\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN0 -> N2, N1\nN12 -> N6, N10\nN4 -> N12, N9\nN10: no outgoing edges\nN13: no outgoing edges\nN5 -> N4, N11\nN3 -> N5, N2\n\nLegal moves: N5, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N5 or N2.  \n- Moving to N5: leads to N4 or N11.  \n  - N4 → N12 or N9; N12 → N6 or N10; N6 → N13 (terminal); N9 → N0; N0 → N2 or N1.  \n  - This path can lead to long chains, but eventually reaches terminal nodes (like N13, N10, N1, N7, N8).  \n  - N11 → N4 or N9, which again loops or leads to terminals.  \n- Moving to N2: leads to N8 (terminal) or N7 (terminal). Both are terminal nodes.  \nThus, moving to N2 immediately ends the game with opponent losing (since opponent has no move from terminal nodes).  \nThis is a winning move. Moving to N5 leads to opponent acting, and while paths exist, they are longer and risk opponent gaining advantage.  \nTherefore, N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N5 or N2.  \n- Moving to N5: leads to N4 or N11.  \n  - N4 → N12 or N9; N12 → N6 or N10; N6 → N13 (terminal); N9 → N0; N0 → N2 or N1.  \n  - This path can lead to long chains, but eventually reaches terminal nodes (like N13, N10, N1, N7, N8).  \n  - N11 → N4 or N9, which again loops or leads to terminals.  \n- Moving to N2: 