[2026-08-18 12:34:26] [agentic_pipeline.py (561)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1863312] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N4, N2\nN7: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N0\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N5, N2\nN4 -> N9, N1\nN8 -> N6, N3\nN3 -> N9, N7\n\nLegal moves: N6, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N6 or N3.  \n- Moving to N6: next player faces N6, which has edges to N4 and N2.  \n  - From N4: edges to N9 and N1.  \n    - N9 and N1 both lead to terminal nodes (N9 has no out, N1 → N5 or N2).  \n    - N1 → N5 (terminal) or N2 → N0 (terminal). So all paths from N4 lead to terminal nodes.  \n  - From N2 → N0 (terminal).  \n  So from N6, opponent is forced into a position where they eventually lose.  \n- Moving to N3: next player faces N3, which has edges to N9 and N7, both terminal nodes.  \n  So opponent immediately loses.  \n\nThus, moving to N3 causes opponent to face a terminal node directly and loses immediately. This is better than N6.  \nTherefore, N3 is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N6 or N3.  \n- Moving to N6: next player faces N6, which has edges to N4 and N2.  \n  - From N4: edges to N9 and N1.  \n    - N9 and N1 both lead to terminal nodes (N9 has no out, N1 → N5 or N2).  \n    - N1 → N5 (terminal) or N2 → N0 (terminal). So all paths from N4 lead to terminal nodes.  \n  - From N2 → N0 (terminal).  \n  So from N6, opponent is forced into a position where they eventually lose.  \n- Moving to N3: next player faces N3, which has edges to N9 and N7, both terminal nodes.  \n  So opponent immediately loses.  \n\nThus, moving to N3 causes opponent to face a terminal node directly and loses immediately. This is better than N6.  \nTherefore, N3 is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N4, N2\nN7: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N0\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N5, N2\nN4 -> N9, N1\nN8 -> N6, N3\nN3 -> N9, N7\n\nLegal moves: N6, N3assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N6 or N3.  \n- Moving to N6: next player faces N6, which has edges to N4 and N2.  \n  - From N4: edges to N9 and N1.  \n    - N9 and N1 both lead to terminal nodes (N9 has no edges, N1 → N5 or N2).  \n    - N1 → N5 (terminal) or N2 → N0 (terminal). So all paths from N4 end with opponent losing.  \n  - From N2: → N0 (terminal). So opponent loses.  \n  Thus, from N6, opponent is forced into a position where they eventually lose.  \n- Moving to N3: next player faces N3 → N9 or N7, both terminal nodes. So opponent immediately loses.  \n\nBoth N6 and N3 lead to opponent