[2026-08-18 12:38:40] [agentic_pipeline.py (561)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1863312] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N3, N7\nN12 -> N10, N5\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N4, N1\nN9 -> N8, N11\nN7 -> N3\nN5 -> N0\nN8 -> N1\nN11 -> N6, N12\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN10: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N10\nN4 -> N2, N9\n\nLegal moves: N4, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N13, the legal moves are to N4 or N1.  \n- Moving to N4: leads to N2 or N9.  \n  - N2 → N3 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n  - N9 → N8 or N11.  \n    - N8 → N1 → N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n    - N11 → N6 (terminal, opponent loses) or N12 → N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n  So from N4, opponent eventually loses.  \n- Moving to N1: leads to N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing immediately. However, N1 leads directly to terminal state in one step. N4 leads to longer path but also ends with opponent losing. Since both are winning, we choose the one that gives immediate win. N1 is simpler and direct. Thus, N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N13, the legal moves are to N4 or N1.  \n- Moving to N4: leads to N2 or N9.  \n  - N2 → N3 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n  - N9 → N8 or N11.  \n    - N8 → N1 → N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n    - N11 → N6 (terminal, opponent loses) or N12 → N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n  So from N4, opponent eventually loses.  \n- Moving to N1: leads to N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing immediately. However, N1 leads directly to terminal state in one step. N4 leads to longer path but also ends with opponent losing. Since both are winning, we choose the one that gives immediate win. N1 is simpler and direct. Thus, N1 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N3, N7\nN12 -> N10, N5\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N4, N1\nN9 -> N8, N11\nN7 -> N3\nN5 -> N0\nN8 -> N1\nN11 -> N6, N12\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN10: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N10\nN4 -> N2, N9\n\nLegal moves: N4, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N13, the legal moves are to N4 or N1.  \n- Moving to N4: leads to N2 or N9.  \n  - N2 → N3 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n  - N9 → N8 or N11.  \n    - N8 → N1 → N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n    - N11 → N6 (terminal, opponent loses) or N12 → N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n  So from N4, opponent eventually loses.  \n- Moving to N1: leads to N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing immediately. However, N1 leads directly to a terminal node in one step. N4 leads to more branching but still ends with opponent losing. Since both are winning, we choose the one that gives immediate loss to opponent. N1 is simpler and direct. Thus, N1 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N13, the legal moves are to N4 or N1.  \n- Moving to N4: leads to N2 or N9.  \n  - N2 → N3 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n  - N9 → N8 or N11.  \n    - N8 → N1 → N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n    - N11 → N6 (terminal, opponent loses) or N12 → N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n  So from N4, opponent eventually loses.  \n- Moving to N1: leads to N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing immediately. However, N1 leads directly to a terminal node in one step. N4 leads to more branching but still ends with opponent losing. Since both are winning, we choose the one that gives immediate loss to opponent. N1 is simpler and direct. Thus, N1 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN2 -> N3, N7\nN12 -> N10, N5\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N4, N1\nN9 -> N8, N11\nN7 -> N3\nN5 -> N0\nN8 -> N1\nN11 -> N6, N12\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN10: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N10\nN4 -> N2, N9\n\nLegal moves: N4, N1assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N13, the legal moves are to N4 or N1.  \n- Moving to N4: From N4, moves are to N2 or N9.  \n  - N2 → N3 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n  - N9 → N8 or N11.  \n    - N8 → N1 → N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n    - N11 → N6 (terminal, opponent loses) or N12 → N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n  So all paths from N4 lead to a terminal node for the opponent.  \n- Moving to N1: From N1 → N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing immediately. However, N13 → N1 is shorter and directly leads to terminal. N13 → N4 leads to more branching but also ends in opponent loss. Either move wins. Since both are winning, choose the one that simplifies control. N1 is direct and secure. Thus, N1 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N1</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N13, the legal moves are to N4 or N1.  \n- Moving to N4: From N4, moves are to N2 or N9.  \n  - N2 → N3 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n  - N9 → N8 or N11.  \n    - N8 → N1 → N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n    - N11 → N6 (terminal, opponent loses) or N12 → N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n  So all paths from N4 lead to a terminal node for the opponent.  \n- Moving to N1: From N1 → N10 (terminal, opponent loses).  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing immediately. However, N13 → N1 is shorter and directly leads to terminal. N13 → N4 leads to more branching but also ends in opponent loss. Either move wins. Since both are winning, choose the one that simplifies control. N1 is direct and secure. Thus, N1 is optimal.</reason>  \n<answer>N1</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this for