urrent node is N1. From N1, legal moves are to N5 or N6.  \n- Moving to N5: next node is N8, then to N9 (terminal), opponent loses. So we win.  \n- Moving to N6: next node is N0 or N4.  \n  - If to N0: opponent loses immediately. Win.  \n  - If to N4: then opponent can go to N2 (lose) or N7 (lose). So opponent loses in either case. Win.  \nThus, both N5 and N6 lead to opponent losing. But we must choose one that maximizes outcome. Since both result in win, either is valid. However, N5 leads to a direct terminal path (N1 → N5 → N8 → N9), which is shorter and more certain. N6 leads to branching but also guarantees opponent loss. Both are winning, but N5 is simpler and more direct. Choose N5.</reason>  \n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Current node is N1. From N1, legal moves are to N5 or N6.  \n- Moving to N5: next node is N8, then to N9 (terminal), opponent loses. So we win.  \n- Moving to N6: next node is N0 or N4.  \n  - If to N0: opponent loses immediately. Win.  \n  - If to N4: then opponent can go to N2 (lose) or N7 (lose). So opponent loses in either case. Win.  \nThus, both N5 and N6 lead to opponent losing. But we must choose one that maximizes outcome. Since both result in win, either is valid. However, N5 leads to a direct terminal path (N1 → N5 → N8 → N9), which is shorter and more certain. N6 leads to branching but also guarantees opponent loss. Both are winning, but N5 is simpler and more direct. Choose N5.</reason>  \n<answer>N5</answer>"}]
