 loses immediately. This is a winning move.  \n- Moving to N3: goes to N7, N10, or N8, all of which have no outgoing edges. Opponent will then move to one of these and lose. So this is also a winning move.  \n- Moving to N13: goes to N2, then from N2 opponent can go to N11, N0, N1, or N5. None of these lead to immediate loss, and opponent can continue. From N1, they can reach N12, N3, N5, N0 — some of which end in terminal nodes. But since N2 leads to branching with multiple options, opponent can potentially force a win. Thus, N13 is less favorable.  \n\nBetween N6 and N3, both lead to opponent losing immediately. N6 leads directly to N9 (terminal), N3 leads to N7, N10, or N8 (all terminal). So either way, opponent loses. Both are winning. However, N6 is a direct terminal move, so it is simpler and more secure. Therefore, N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N13\n\nDirected graph:\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN7 -> N2, N8, N6, N9\nN10 -> N12\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N5, N4, N6\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N11\nN4 -> N0\nN11 -> N1, N5\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N11, N3, N10\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN5 -> N7\n\nLegal moves: N11, N3, N10assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Current node is N13. From N13, legal moves are to N11, N3, or N10.  \n- Moving to N11: next player faces N11, which has edges to N1 and N5. From there, opponen