[2026-08-18 13:12:00] [agentic_pipeline.py (561)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1863312] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN10 -> N8\nN1 -> N0, N2, N6\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N7\nN7 -> N13, N9, N11\nN0 -> N3, N6, N7, N2\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN11 -> N4\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N10\nN9 -> N12\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N5\n\nLegal moves: N0, N2, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the available moves are to N0, N2, or N6. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N0: From N0, moves to N3 (loss), N6, N7, or N2. N3 and N5 have no outgoing edges, so if opponent reaches N3 or N5, they lose. But N6 leads to N5 (loss), N7 leads to N13→N10→N8 (loss), N2 leads to N7→... which eventually loops or leads to N8 or N13. The path N0 → N7 → N13 → N10 → N8 ends with opponent losing. So N0 leads to a winning position.\n\n- Move to N2: N2 → N7 → N13 → N10 → N8 (opponent loses). So this is also a winning move.\n\n- Move to N6: N6 → N5 (no outgoing edges, opponent loses immediately). This is a direct win.\n\nThus, moving to N6 wins immediately. This is the best outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the available moves are to N0, N2, or N6. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N0: From N0, moves to N3 (loss), N6, N7, or N2. N3 and N5 have no outgoing edges, so if opponent reaches N3 or N5, they lose. But N6 leads to N5 (loss), N7 leads to N13→N10→N8 (loss), N2 leads to N7→... which eventually loops or leads to N8 or N13. The path N0 → N7 → N13 → N10 → N8 ends with opponent losing. So N0 leads to a winning position.\n\n- Move to N2: N2 → N7 → N13 → N10 → N8 (opponent loses). So this is also a winning move.\n\n- Move to N6: N6 → N5 (no outgoing edges, opponent loses immediately). This is a direct win.\n\nThus, moving to N6 wins immediately. This is the best outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN10 -> N8\nN1 -> N0, N2, N6\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N7\nN7 -> N13, N9, N11\nN0 -> N3, N6, N7, N2\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN11 -> N4\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N10\nN9 -> N12\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N5\n\nLegal moves: N0, N2, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the available moves are to N0, N2, or N6. We analyze each to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a terminal node with no outgoing edges).  \n- Move to N0: From N0, moves go to N3 (terminal), N6, N7, N2. N3 and N6 lead to N5 (terminal) or N2 → N7 → N13 → N10 → N8 (terminal). N7 leads to N13 → N10 → N8 (terminal). So all paths from N0 eventually lead to a terminal node for the opponent.  \n- Move to N2: N2 → N7 → N13 → N10 → N8 (terminal). Opponent loses.  \n- Move to N6: N6 → N5 (terminal). Opponent loses immediately.  \n\nAll moves win, but we must choose one that maximizes outcome. Since N6 leads to immediate terminal node (N5), the opponent has no move and loses instantly. This is the strongest move. Thus, N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the available moves are to N0, N2, or N6. We analyze each to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a terminal node with no outgoing edges).  \n- Move to N0: From N0, moves go to N3 (terminal), N6, N7, N2. N3 and N6 lead to N5 (terminal) or N2 → N7 → N13 → N10 → N8 (terminal). N7 leads to N13 → N10 → N8 (terminal). So all paths from N0 eventually lead to a terminal node for the opponent.  \n- Move to N2: N2 → N7 → N13 → N10 → N8 (terminal). Opponent loses.  \n- Move to N6: N6 → N5 (terminal). Opponent loses immediately.  \n\nAll moves win, but we must choose one that maximizes outcome. Since N6 leads to immediate terminal node (N5), the opponent has no move and loses instantly. This is the strongest move. Thus, N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN10 -> N8\nN1 -> N0, N2, N6\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N7\nN7 -> N13, N9, N11\nN0 -> N3, N6, N7, N2\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN11 -> N4\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N10\nN9 -> N12\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N5\n\nLegal moves: N0, N2, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the available moves are to N0, N2, or N6. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N0: From N0, outgoing edges go to N3, N6, N7, N2. N3 and N5 are terminal (no outgoing edges), so if opponent reaches N3 or N5, they lose. But from N7, they can go to N13, N9, N11. N13 → N10 → N8 (terminal), N9 → N12 (terminal), N11 → N4 (terminal). So opponent can force a win from N0.  \n- Move to N2: From N2 → N7 → leads to N13, N9, N11, all of which eventually lead to terminal nodes. Opponent can win from here too.  \n- Move to N6: From N6 → N5 (terminal). Opponent has no moves and loses immediately. This is a winning move.\n\nThus, moving to N6 forces the opponent into a terminal node with no outgoing edges, so they lose. This is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the available moves are to N0, N2, or N6. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N0: From N0, outgoing edges go to N3, N6, N7, N2. N3 and N5 are terminal (no outgoing edges), so if opponent reaches N3 or N5, they lose. But from N7, they can go to N13, N9, N11. N13 → N10 → N8 (terminal), N9 → N12 (terminal), N11 → N4 (terminal). So opponent can force a win from N0.  \n- Move to N2: From N2 → N7 → leads to N13, N9, N11, all of which eventually lead to terminal nodes. Opponent can win from here too.  \n- Move to N6: From N6 → N5 (terminal). Opponent has no moves and loses immediately. This is a winning move.\n\nThus, moving to N6 forces the opponent into a terminal node with no outgoing edges, so they lose. This is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN10 -> N8\nN1 -> N0, N2, N6\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N7\nN7 -> N13, N9, N11\nN0 -> N3, N6, N7, N2\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN11 -> N4\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N10\nN9 -> N12\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N5\n\nLegal moves: N0, N2, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the available moves are to N0, N2, or N6. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N0: From N0, moves to N3 (loss), N6, N7, or N2. N3 and N5 have no outgoing edges, so if opponent reaches N3 or N5, they lose. But from N7, opponent can go to N13, N9, N11. N13 → N10 → N8 (dead end), N9 → N12 (dead end), N11 → N4 (dead end). So all paths from N7 lead to dead ends. Thus, if we move to N0, opponent can force a win by going to N7, then to N13/N9/N11, all ending in terminal nodes. So opponent wins.\n\n- Move to N2: From N2 → N7, then opponent can go to N13, N9, N11 — all lead to dead ends. So opponent wins.\n\n- Move to N6: From N6 → N5 (dead end). So opponent loses immediately. This is a winning move.\n\nThus, moving to N6 forces opponent to face a terminal node (N5) and loses. This is the best move.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the available moves are to N0, N2, or N6. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N0: From N0, moves to N3 (loss), N6, N7, or N2. N3 and N5 have no outgoing edges, so if opponent reaches N3 or N5, they lose. But from N7, opponent can go to N13, N9, N11. N13 → N10 → N8 (dead end), N9 → N12 (dead end), N11 → N4 (dead end). So all paths from N7 lead to dead ends. Thus, if we move to N0, opponent can force a win by going to N7, then to N13/N9/N11, all ending in terminal nodes. So opponent wins.\n\n- Move to N2: From N2 → N7, then opponent can go to N13, N9, N11 — all lead to dead ends. So opponent wins.\n\n- Move to N6: From N6 → N5 (dead end). So opponent loses immediately. This is a winning move.\n\nThus, moving to N6 forces opponent to face a terminal node (N5) and loses. This is the best move.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN10 -> N8\nN1 -> N0, N2, N6\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N7\nN7 -> N13, N9, N11\nN0 -> N3, N6, N7, N2\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN11 -> N4\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N10\nN9 -> N12\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N5\n\nLegal moves: N0, N2, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the available moves are to N0, N2, or N6. We analyze each to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a terminal node with no outgoing edges).  \n- Move to N0: From N0, moves go to N3 (terminal), N6, N7, N2. N3 and N6 lead to further moves. N7 leads to N13, N9, N11. N13 → N10 → N8 (terminal), N9 → N12 (terminal), N11 → N4 (terminal). So from N0, opponent can eventually be forced to a terminal node.  \n- Move to N2: N2 → N7 → N13, N9, N11. All lead to terminal nodes (N13→N10→N8, N9→N12, N11→N4). So opponent loses immediately.  \n- Move to N6: N6 → N5 (terminal). Opponent loses immediately.  \n\nBoth N2 and N6 lead to immediate opponent loss. But N6 leads directly to N5 (terminal), so opponent has no move. N2 leads to N7, which has multiple paths, but eventually all lead to terminal nodes. However, N6 is simpler and directly ends the game. Since both are winning, we choose the one that maximizes control — N6 is safer and direct.  \n\nThus, N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the available moves are to N0, N2, or N6. We analyze each to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a terminal node with no outgoing edges).  \n- Move to N0: From N0, moves go to N3 (terminal), N6, N7, N2. N3 and N6 lead to further moves. N7 leads to N13, N9, N11. N13 → N10 → N8 (terminal), N9 → N12 (terminal), N11 → N4 (terminal). So from N0, opponent can eventually be forced to a terminal node.  \n- Move to N2: N2 → N7 → N13, N9, N11. All lead to terminal nodes (N13→N10→N8, N9→N12, N11→N4). So opponent loses immediately.  \n- Move to N6: N6 → N5 (terminal). Opponent loses immediately.  \n\nBoth N2 and N6 lead to immediate opponent loss. But N6 leads directly to N5 (terminal), so opponent has no move. N2 leads to N7, which has multiple paths, but eventually all lead to terminal nodes. However, N6 is simpler and directly ends the game. Since both are winning, we choose the one that maximizes control — N6 is safer and direct.  \n\nThus, N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that do