es to N0 and N11 (both dead ends). So opponent loses immediately.  \n\nThus, moving to N5 forces the opponent into a dead end and wins the game.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN10 -> N8\nN11: no outgoing edges\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N2, N11\nN9 -> N2, N10\nN2 -> N3, N4, N12\nN13: no outgoing edges\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N9, N7, N6\nN7 -> N9\nN6 -> N5, N13\nN4 -> N0, N11\n\nLegal moves: N9, N7, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9, N7, or N6. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N9: Then opponent is at N9, which has edges to N2 and N10. If opponent goes to N2, they reach N3, N4, or N12 — all of which eventually lead to terminal nodes (no outgoing edges) or loops. But from N2, N4 leads to N0 or N11 (both terminal), so opponent can force a win. If opponent goes to N10, then to N8, then to N2 or N11 — again, N11 is terminal. So opponent can win from N9.\n\n- Move to N7: Opponent at N7 → N9 → same as above, opponent can win.\n\n- Move to N6: Opponent at N6 → N5 or N13, both terminal nodes (no outgoing edges). So opponent loses immediately.\n\nThus, moving to N6 forces opponent into a terminal node and wins the game.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9, N7, or 