so loses immediately.  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing. But from N7, opponent has an immediate losing move (to N1). From N2, opponent must choose between N5 or N4, both leading to terminal nodes. Either way, opponent loses.  \n\nHowever, since the goal is to maximize outcome, and both options lead to opponent losing, either is valid. But choosing N7 gives opponent a direct terminal option (N1), which is stronger. Still, both are winning. But since we want to maximize outcome, and no difference in outcome, pick the one that gives more control. N7 gives immediate terminal option.  \n\nThus, N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N2 or N7.  \n- Moving to N2: next player is at N2, which has edges to N5 and N4.  \n  - If opponent goes to N5: leads to N1 or N6 (both terminal). Opponent loses.  \n  - If opponent goes to N4: leads to N9 or N8 (both terminal). Opponent loses.  \n  So from N2, opponent has no winning move — player wins.  \n- Moving to N7: next player is at N7, which has edges to N1 and N5.  \n  - N1 is terminal → opponent loses immediately.  \n  - N5 leads to N1 or N6 (both terminal).  \n  So from N7, opponent also loses immediately.  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing. But from N7, opponent has an immediate losing move (to N1). From N2, opponent must choose between N5 or N4, both leading to terminal nodes. Either way, opponent loses.  \n\nHowever, since the goal is to maximize outcome, and both options lead to opponent losing, either is valid. But choosing N7 gives opponent a direct terminal option (N1), which is stronger. Still, both are winning. But since we want to maximize outcome, and no difference in outcome, pick the one that gives more control. N7 gives immediate terminal option.  \n\nThus, N7 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N7</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate movin