ads to N5 or N2; N2 ends opponent’s turn (opponent loses).  \n  - If opponent goes to N4: leads to N0 (opponent loses).  \n  So from N6, opponent has no winning move — opponent loses immediately. So this is a winning move.\n\n- Move to N8: opponent at N8 → can go to N5 or N2.  \n  - N2: opponent loses.  \n  - N5 → N7 (opponent loses).  \n  So opponent loses in either case. Also a winning move.\n\n- Move to N9: opponent at N9 → goes to N4 → N0 (opponent loses). Also a winning move.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing. Any choice wins. Choose one that maximizes outcome — all are equivalent. Pick N6 as it is first and leads to clear loss for opponent.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N1, N8, N4\nN9 -> N4\nN5 -> N7\nN7: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N6, N8, N9\nN4 -> N0\nN8 -> N5, N2\nN2: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N6, N8, N9assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N6, N8, or N9. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N6: Then opponent is at N6, which has edges to N1, N8, N4.  \n  - If opponent goes to N1 (dead end), they lose.  \n  - If to N8: leads to N5 or N2; N2 is dead end, so opponent can go to N5 → N7 (dead end), so opponent loses.  \n  - If to N4 → N0 (dead end), opponent loses.  \n  So from N6, opponent has no winning move — all paths lead to opponent losing. So N6 is winning.\n\n- Move to N8: opponent at N8 → can go to N5 or N2.  \n  - N2 is dead end → opponent loses.  \n  - N5 → N7 (dead end) → opponent loses.  \n  So all opponent moves lead to loss. So N8 is winning.\n\n- Move to N9: opponent at N9 → goes to N4 → N0 (dead end) → opponent loses. So N9 is winning.\n\nAll moves from N3 lead to opponent losing. Thus, any move is winning. Choose one that maximizes outcome — all are equivalent. Pick N6 as it is a direct move and leads to strong control. </reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N3, the legal moves are to N6, N8, or N9. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N6: Then opponent is at N6, which has edges to N1, N8, N4.  \n  - If opponent goes to N1 (dead end), they lose.  \n  - If to N8: leads to N5 or N2; N2 is dead end, so opponent can go to N5 → N7 (dead end), so opponent loses.  \n  - If to N4 → N0 (dead end), opponent loses.  \n  So from N6, opponent has no winning move — all paths lead to opponent losing. So N6 is winning.\n\n- Move to N8: opponent at N8 → can go to N5 or N2.  \n  - N2 is dead end → opponent loses.  \n  - N5 → N7 (dead end) → opponent loses.  \n  So all opponent moves lead to loss. So N8 is winning.\n\n- Move to N9: opponent at N9 → goes to N4 → N0 (dead end) → opponent loses. So N9 is winning.\n\nAll moves from N3 lead to opponent losing. Thus, any move is winning. Choose one that maximizes outcome — all are equivalent. Pick N6 as it is a direct move and leads to strong control. </reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}]
