2 and N1.  \n  - N12 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately. So opponent loses → I win.  \n- Moving to N8: next node is N8 → edge to N11.  \n  - N11 → N0 or N13.  \n    - N0 → N9 or N10  \n    - N13 → N5 or N9  \n    - N5 and N3 have no outgoing edges → opponent can eventually be forced to a dead end.  \n    But from N11, opponent can go to N0 → N9 → N12 (dead end) or N10 → N3 or N4.  \n    N4 → N5 or N2 (both dead ends). So opponent can eventually force a win.  \nThus, moving to N6 leads to immediate win (opponent faces N12, no moves). Moving to N8 leads to a longer game with opponent having more options. Therefore, N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Current node is N7. From N7, legal moves are to N6 or N8.  \n- Moving to N6: next node is N6 → edges to N12 and N1.  \n  - N12 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately. So opponent loses → I win.  \n- Moving to N8: next node is N8 → edge to N11.  \n  - N11 → N0 or N13.  \n    - N0 → N9 or N10  \n    - N13 → N5 or N9  \n    - N5 and N3 have no outgoing edges → opponent can eventually be forced to a dead end.  \n    But from N11, opponent can go to N0 → N9 → N12 (dead end) or N10 → N3 or N4.  \n    N4 → N5 or N2 (both dead ends). So opponent can eventually force a win.  \nThus, moving to N6 leads to immediate win (opponent faces N12, no moves). Moving to N8 leads to a longer game with opponent having more options. Therefore, N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}]
