g edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N4, N3\nN1 -> N9, N6\nN6 -> N0\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N5, N8\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN7 -> N2, N3\nN4 -> N8, N7\n\nLegal moves: N9, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the current player can move to N9 or N6. Moving to N6 leads to N0 (a terminal node), so the opponent loses immediately. Thus, moving to N6 wins the game. Moving to N9 leads to N4 or N3, both of which eventually lead to terminal nodes (N8, N5, N2) or cycles, but N6 is a direct winning move. Therefore, the best move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the current player can move to N9 or N6. Moving to N6 leads to N0 (a terminal node), so the opponent loses immediately. Thus, moving to N6 wins the game. Moving to N9 leads to N4 or N3, both of which eventually lead to terminal nodes (N8, N5, N2) or cycles, but N6 is a direct winning move. Therefore, the best move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N4, N3\nN1 -> N9, N6\nN6 -> N0\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N5, N8\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN7 -> N2, N3\nN4 -> N8, N7\n\nLegal moves: N9, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the current player can move to N9 or N6. Moving to N6 leads to N0 (a terminal node), so the opponent loses immediately. Moving to N9 leads to N4 or N3. From N4, the opponent can go to N8 (loss) or N7. From N7, opponent can go to N2 (loss) or N3. From N3, opponent can go to N5 (loss) or N8 (loss). Thus, any move from N9 eventually leads to a terminal node for the opponent. Moving to N6 immediately ends the game with opponent losing. Therefore, moving to N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the current player can move to N9 or N6. Moving to N6 leads to N0 (a terminal node), so the opponent loses immediately. Moving to N9 leads to N4 or N3. From N4, the opponent can go to N8 (loss) or N7. From N7, opponent can go to N2 (loss) or N3. From N3, opponent can go to N5 (loss) or N8 (loss). Thus, any move from N9 eventually leads to a terminal node for the opponent. Moving to N6 immediately ends the game with opponent losing. Therefore, moving to N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N4, N3\nN1 -> N9, N6\nN6 -> N0\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N5, N8\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN7 -> N2, N3\nN4 -> N8, N7\n\nLegal moves: N9, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the current player can move to N9 or N6. Moving to N6 leads to N0 (a terminal node), so the opponent loses immediately. Moving to N9 leads to N4 or N3. From N4, the opponent can go to N8 (loss) or N7. From N7, opponent can go to N2 (loss) or N3. From N3, opponent can go to N5 (loss) or N8 (loss). Thus, any move from N9 eventually leads to a terminal node for the opponent. Similarly, N6 leads directly to N0, a terminal node. Therefore, moving to N6 wins immediately. N6 is the best move.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the current player can move to N9 or N6. Moving to N6 leads to N0 (a terminal node), so the opponent loses immediately. Moving to N9 leads to N4 or N3. From N4, the opponent can go to N8 (loss) or N7. From N7, opponent can go to N2 (loss) or N3. From N3, opponent can go to N5 (loss) or N8 (loss). Thus, any move from N9 eventually leads to a terminal node for the opponent. Similarly, N6 leads directly to N0, a terminal node. Therefore, moving to N6 wins immediately. N6 is the best move.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N4, N3\nN1 -> N9, N6\nN6 -> N0\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N5, N8\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN7 -> N2, N3\nN4 -> N8, N7\n\nLegal moves: N9, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9 or N6.  \n- Moving to N9: then opponent is at N9, which has edges to N4 and N3.  \n  - If opponent goes to N4, they reach N8 or N7.  \n    - N7 leads to N2 or N3; N3 leads to N5 or N8 (both terminal).  \n    - N8 and N5 are terminal (opponent loses).  \n  - If opponent goes to N3, they go to N5 or N8 (both terminal).  \n  So from N9, opponent can force a win by moving to N3 or N4.  \n- Moving to N6: opponent goes to N0 (terminal), so opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N6 wins the game.</reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9 or N6.  \n- Moving to N9: then opponent is at N9, which has edges to N4 and N3.  \n  - If opponent goes to N4, they reach N8 or N7.  \n    - N7 leads to N2 or N3; N3 leads to N5 or N8 (both terminal).  \n    - N8 and N5 are terminal (opponent loses).  \n  - If opponent goes to N3, they go to N5 or N8 (both terminal).  \n  So from N9, opponent can force a win by moving to N3 or N4.  \n- Moving to N6: opponent goes to N0 (terminal), so opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N6 wins the game.</reason>  \n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N4, N3\nN1 -> N9, N6\nN6 -> N0\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N5, N8\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN7 -> N2, N3\nN4 -> N8, N7\n\nLegal moves: N9, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9 or N6. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N9: Then opponent is at N9, which has edges to N4 and N3.\n  - If opponent goes to N4, they reach N8 or N7.\n    - N7 leads to N2 or N3; N3 leads to N5 or N8 (both terminal).\n    - N8 and N5 are terminal (opponent loses).\n  - If opponent goes to N3, they reach N5 or N8 (both terminal).\n  - So from N9, opponent can force a win by moving to N3 or N4, both leading to terminal nodes.\n\n- Move to N6: Then opponent is at N6, which goes to N0 (terminal). So opponent loses immediately.\n\nThus, moving to N6 causes the opponent to lose immediately. This is a winning move.\n\nTherefore, the optimal first move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9 or N6. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N9: Then opponent is at N9, which has edges to N4 and N3.\n  - If opponent goes to N4, they reach N8 or N7.\n    - N7 leads to N2 or N3; N3 leads to N5 or N8 (both terminal).\n    - N8 and N5 are terminal (opponent loses).\n  - If opponent goes to N3, they reach N5 or N8 (both terminal).\n  - So from N9, opponent can force a win by moving to N3 or N4, both leading to terminal nodes.\n\n- Move to N6: Then opponent is at N6, which goes to N0 (terminal). So opponent loses immediately.\n\nThus, moving to N6 causes the opponent to lose immediately. This is a winning move.\n\nTherefore, the optimal first move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N4, N3\nN1 -> N9, N6\nN6 -> N0\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N5, N8\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN7 -> N2, N3\nN4 -> N8, N7\n\nLegal moves: N9, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9 or N6. We analyze both options to determine which leads to a winning position.\n\nIf we move to N9:  \nFrom N9, moves are to N4 or N3.  \n- N4 → N8 or N7  \n  - N8 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately.  \n  - N7 → N2 or N3  \n    - N2 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses.  \n    - N3 → N5 or N8 → both lead to opponent losing eventually.  \nSo from N9, the player can force a win by moving to N4 → N8 or N7 → N2 or N3, all of which end with opponent losing.\n\nIf we move to N6:  \nFrom N6 → N0 (no outgoing edges) → opponent loses immediately. So this is a winning move.\n\nMoving to N6 leads to immediate opponent loss. Thus, N6 is better than N9.\n\nTherefore, the optimal first move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9 or N6. We analyze both options to determine which leads to a winning position.\n\nIf we move to N9:  \nFrom N9, moves are to N4 or N3.  \n- N4 → N8 or N7  \n  - N8 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately.  \n  - N7 → N2 or N3  \n    - N2 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses.  \n    - N3 → N5 or N8 → both lead to opponent losing eventually.  \nSo from N9, the player can force a win by moving to N4 → N8 or N7 → N2 or N3, all of which end with opponent losing.\n\nIf we move to N6:  \nFrom N6 → N0 (no outgoing edges) → opponent loses immediately. So this is a winning move.\n\nMoving to N6 leads to immediate opponent loss. Thus, N6 is better than N9.\n\nTherefore, the optimal first move is to N6.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN9 -> N4, N3\nN1 -> N9, N6\nN6 -> N0\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN3 -> N5, N8\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN7 -> N2, N3\nN4 -> N8, N7\n\nLegal moves: N9, N6assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9 or N6. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N9: From N9, moves to N4 or N3.\n  - N4 → N8 or N7\n    - N8: no outgoing edges → opponent loses → we win.\n    - N7 → N2 or N3\n      - N2: no outgoing edges → opponent loses → we win.\n      - N3 → N5 or N8 → both lead to opponent losing eventually.\n  - N3 → N5 or N8 → both lead to opponent losing.\n  So all paths from N9 eventually lead to opponent losing.\n\n- Move to N6: From N6 → N0 (no outgoing edges) → opponent loses immediately → we win.\n\nBoth moves lead to win, but moving to N6 ends the game immediately. Moving to N9 leads to a longer game but still win. Since we want to maximize outcome and both are winning, the safest and most direct win is to go to N6, which immediately ends the game with opponent losing.\n\nThus, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N1, the legal moves are to N9 or N6. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N9: From N9, moves to N4 or N3.\n  - N4 → N8 or N7\n    - N8: no outgoing edges → opponent loses → we win.\n    - N7 → N2 or N3\n      - N2: no outgoing edges → opponent loses → we win.\n      - N3 → N5 or N8 → both lead to opponent losing eventually.\n  - N3 → N5 or N8 → both lead to opponent losing.\n  So all paths from N9 eventually lead to opponent losing.\n\n- Move to N6: From N6 → N0 (no outgoing edges) → opponent loses immediately → we win.\n\nBoth moves lead to win, but moving to N6 ends the game immediately. Moving to N9 leads to a longer game but still win. Since we want to maximize outcome and both are winning, the safest and most direct win is to go to N6, which immediately ends the game with opponent losing.\n\nThus, choosing N6 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N6</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N1, N6\nN11: no outgoing edges\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN0 -> N11\nN1 -> N4, N10\nN2 -> N13, N7, N6\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN10: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N0, N12\nN6 -> N8\nN5 -> N2\nN8 -> N9\nN3 -> N5, N2, N13\n\nLegal moves: N5, N2, N13assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Current node is N3. From N3, legal moves are to N5, N2, or N13. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N5: goes to N2. From N2, options are N13, N7, N6.\n  - N13 → N0 → N11 (terminal, opponent loses).\n  - N7 → N1 → N4 or N10 (both terminal).\n  - N6 → N8 → N9 (terminal).\n  All paths eventually lead to a terminal node (no outgoing edges), so opponent loses. This is a winning move.\n\n- Move to N2: same as above, leads to N13, N7, N6 — all eventually terminal. Opponent loses.\n\n- Move to N13: goes to N0 → N11 (terminal). Opponent loses.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing. Any choice wins. Choose one that maximizes outcome — all are equivalent. Pick N5 as it leads to N2, which has multiple options, ensuring control. Final outcome is win.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Current node is N3. From N3, legal moves are to N5, N2, or N13. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N5: goes to N2. From N2, options are N13, N7, N6.\n  - N13 → N0 → N11 (terminal, opponent loses).\n  - N7 → N1 → N4 or N10 (both terminal).\n  - N6 → N8 → N9 (terminal).\n  All paths eventually lead to a terminal node (no outgoing edges), so opponent loses. This is a winning move.\n\n- Move to N2: same as above, leads to N13, N7, N6 — all eventually terminal. Opponent loses.\n\n- Move to N13: goes to N0 → N11 (terminal). Opponent loses.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing. Any choice wins. Choose one that maximizes outcome — all are equivalent. Pick N5 as it leads to N2, which has multiple options, ensuring control. Final outcome is win.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N3\n\nDirected graph:\nN7 -> N1, N6\nN11: no outgoing edges\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN0 -> N11\nN1 -> N4, N10\nN2 -> N13, N7, N6\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN12: no outgoing edges\nN10: no outgoing edges\nN13 -> N0, N12\nN6 -> N8\nN5 -> N2\nN8 -> N9\nN3 -> N5, N2, N13\n\nLegal moves: N5, N2, N13assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Current node is N3. From N3, legal moves are to N5, N2, or N13. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N5: goes to N2. From N2, moves are to N13, N7, N6.\n  - If go to N13: N13 → N0 → N11 (terminal, opponent loses).\n  - If go to N7: N7 → N1 → N4 (opponent loses).\n  - If go to N6: N6 → N8 → N9 (opponent loses).\n  All paths from N2 lead to opponent losing. So N5 leads to win.\n\n- Move to N2: same as above, leads to opponent losing.\n\n- Move to N13: N13 → N0 → N11 (opponent loses).\n\nAll moves from N3 lead to opponent losing. Thus, any move wins. Choose one that maximizes outcome — all are equivalent. Pick N5 as it leads to a strong path.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>", "episode_score": 1.05, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Current node is N3. From N3, legal moves are to N5, N2, or N13. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N5: goes to N2. From N2, moves are to N13, N7, N6.\n  - If go to N13: N13 → N0 → N11 (terminal, opponent loses).\n  - If go to N7: N7 → N1 → N4 (opponent loses).\n  - If go to N6: N6 → N8 → N9 (opponent loses).\n  All paths from N2 lead to opponent losing. So N5 leads to win.\n\n- Move to N2: same as above, leads to opponent losing.\n\n- Move to N13: N13 → N0 → N11 (opponent loses).\n\nAll moves from N3 lead to opponent losing. Thus, any move wins. Choose one that maximizes outcome — all are equivalent. Pick N5 as it leads to a strong path.</reason>\n<answer>N5</answer>"}]
