[2026-08-18 13:53:45] [agentic_pipeline.py (561)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1863312] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN4 -> N6, N1\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N9\nN7 -> N1, N0\nN3 -> N2\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN5 -> N3, N7, N0\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN0 -> N8, N4\n\nLegal moves: N3, N7, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N3, N7, or N0. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N3: goes to N2, which has no outgoing edges. Opponent loses immediately. This is a winning move.\n\n- Move to N7: goes to N1, then to N9 (no outgoing edges), opponent loses. Also a winning move.\n\n- Move to N0: goes to N8 (no outgoing edges), opponent loses. Also a winning move.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing immediately. Any of these is winning. However, we must choose one that maximizes outcome. Since all result in immediate win, any is optimal. Choose N3 as it is simplest and directly leads to terminal node.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N3, N7, or N0. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N3: goes to N2, which has no outgoing edges. Opponent loses immediately. This is a winning move.\n\n- Move to N7: goes to N1, then to N9 (no outgoing edges), opponent loses. Also a winning move.\n\n- Move to N0: goes to N8 (no outgoing edges), opponent loses. Also a winning move.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing immediately. Any of these is winning. However, we must choose one that maximizes outcome. Since all result in immediate win, any is optimal. Choose N3 as it is simplest and directly leads to terminal node.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN4 -> N6, N1\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N9\nN7 -> N1, N0\nN3 -> N2\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN5 -> N3, N7, N0\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN0 -> N8, N4\n\nLegal moves: N3, N7, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N3, N7, or N0. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N3: goes to N2 (no outgoing edges), opponent loses immediately. So we win.\n\n- Move to N7: goes to N1, then N9 (no outgoing edges), opponent loses. So we win.\n\n- Move to N0: goes to N8 or N4. \n  - N8 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately.\n  - N4 → N6 or N1; N6 has no outgoing edges, so if opponent moves to N6, they lose. But from N4, opponent can go to N1 → N9 (opponent loses). So opponent still eventually loses.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing eventually. However, the first move to N3 leads to N2 (immediate loss for opponent), which is a win. This is the most direct and secure win. Thus, N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N3, N7, or N0. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N3: goes to N2 (no outgoing edges), opponent loses immediately. So we win.\n\n- Move to N7: goes to N1, then N9 (no outgoing edges), opponent loses. So we win.\n\n- Move to N0: goes to N8 or N4. \n  - N8 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately.\n  - N4 → N6 or N1; N6 has no outgoing edges, so if opponent moves to N6, they lose. But from N4, opponent can go to N1 → N9 (opponent loses). So opponent still eventually loses.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing eventually. However, the first move to N3 leads to N2 (immediate loss for opponent), which is a win. This is the most direct and secure win. Thus, N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN4 -> N6, N1\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N9\nN7 -> N1, N0\nN3 -> N2\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN5 -> N3, N7, N0\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN0 -> N8, N4\n\nLegal moves: N3, N7, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N3, N7, or N0. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N3: goes to N2, which has no outgoing edges. Opponent loses immediately. This is a winning move.\n\n- Move to N7: goes to N1, then to N9 (no outgoing edges). Opponent loses after their turn. Also a winning move.\n\n- Move to N0: goes to N8 or N4. N8 has no outgoing edges, so if opponent is at N8, they lose. But if they go to N4, they can then go to N6 or N1. N6 has no outgoing edges, so if opponent reaches N6, they lose. However, from N4, opponent can go to N6 (lose) or N1 (then to N9 — lose). So from N0, opponent is forced into a losing position regardless. Also a winning move.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing. But we want to maximize outcome. Since all are winning, we pick one. N3 leads directly to a terminal node (N2) — immediate win. This is simplest and most direct. Thus, N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N3, N7, or N0. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N3: goes to N2, which has no outgoing edges. Opponent loses immediately. This is a winning move.\n\n- Move to N7: goes to N1, then to N9 (no outgoing edges). Opponent loses after their turn. Also a winning move.\n\n- Move to N0: goes to N8 or N4. N8 has no outgoing edges, so if opponent is at N8, they lose. But if they go to N4, they can then go to N6 or N1. N6 has no outgoing edges, so if opponent reaches N6, they lose. However, from N4, opponent can go to N6 (lose) or N1 (then to N9 — lose). So from N0, opponent is forced into a losing position regardless. Also a winning move.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing. But we want to maximize outcome. Since all are winning, we pick one. N3 leads directly to a terminal node (N2) — immediate win. This is simplest and most direct. Thus, N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N5\n\nDirected graph:\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN4 -> N6, N1\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N9\nN7 -> N1, N0\nN3 -> N2\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN5 -> N3, N7, N0\nN2: no outgoing edges\nN0 -> N8, N4\n\nLegal moves: N3, N7, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N3, N7, or N0. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N3: goes to N2 (no outgoing edges), opponent loses immediately → win.\n- Move to N7: goes to N1, then N9 (no outgoing edges), opponent loses → win.\n- Move to N0: goes to N8 or N4. \n  - N8 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately if they go to N8.\n  - N4 → N6 or N1; N6 has no outgoing edges, so if opponent goes to N6, they lose.\n  - But from N0, opponent can choose N8 (immediate loss) or N4 (then they continue). If they go to N4, they can go to N6 (lose) or N1 → N9 (lose). So all paths from N0 eventually lead to a terminal node for opponent.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing eventually. However, moving to N3 leads directly to N2 (no outgoing edges), so opponent has no move and loses immediately. This is the fastest and most certain win. Thus, N3 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N3</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N5, the legal moves are to N3, N7, or N0. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N3: goes to N2 (no outgoing edges), opponent loses immediately → win.\n- Move to N7: goes to N1, then N9 (no outgoing edges), opponent loses → win.\n- Move to N0: goes to N8 or N4. \n  - N8 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately if they go to N8.\n  - N4 → N6 or N1; N6 has no outgoing edges, so if opponent goes to N6, they lose.\n  - But from N0, opponent can choose N8 (immediate loss) or N4 (then they continue). If they go to N4, they can go to N6 (lose) or N1 → N9 (lose). So all paths from N0 eventually lead to a terminal node for opponent.\n\nAll moves lead to opponent losing eventually. However, moving