[2026-08-18 14:01:07] [agentic_pipeline.py (561)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1863312] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N4, N1\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN9 -> N8, N2\nN4 -> N1, N6\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN7: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N5, N3\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N5, N7\nN3 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N8, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N2.  \n- Moving to N8: next player is at N8, which has edges to N4 and N1.  \n  - If opponent goes to N4, they reach N1 → N5 (dead end) or N6 (dead end).  \n  - If opponent goes to N1, they reach N5 (dead end) or N3 → N0 (dead end).  \n  - So from N8, opponent can force a win by moving to N4 or N1, both leading to dead ends.  \n- Moving to N2: next player is at N2, which has edges to N5 (dead end) and N7 (dead end).  \n  - Both are dead ends, so opponent has no legal move and loses immediately.  \n\nThus, moving to N2 forces the opponent into a position with no outgoing edges, so they lose. This is a winning move.  \n</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N2.  \n- Moving to N8: next player is at N8, which has edges to N4 and N1.  \n  - If opponent goes to N4, they reach N1 → N5 (dead end) or N6 (dead end).  \n  - If opponent goes to N1, they reach N5 (dead end) or N3 → N0 (dead end).  \n  - So from N8, opponent can force a win by moving to N4 or N1, both leading to dead ends.  \n- Moving to N2: next player is at N2, which has edges to N5 (dead end) and N7 (dead end).  \n  - Both are dead ends, so opponent has no legal move and loses immediately.  \n\nThus, moving to N2 forces the opponent into a position with no outgoing edges, so they lose. This is a winning move.  \n</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N4, N1\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN9 -> N8, N2\nN4 -> N1, N6\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN7: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N5, N3\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N5, N7\nN3 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N8, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N2.  \n- Moving to N8: next player is at N8, which has edges to N4 and N1.  \n  - From N4: moves to N1 or N6.  \n    - N6 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately. So if we go N9 → N8 → N4 → N6, opponent loses.  \n    - N1 → N5 or N3; N5 ends with no outgoing edges, so opponent loses.  \n    - N3 → N0, which has no outgoing edges → opponent loses.  \n  So from N8, opponent can be forced to a terminal node.  \n- Moving to N2: next player is at N2 → edges to N5 or N7.  \n  - N5 and N7 both have no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N2 causes opponent to lose immediately. This is a winning move.  \nTherefore, choosing N2 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N2.  \n- Moving to N8: next player is at N8, which has edges to N4 and N1.  \n  - From N4: moves to N1 or N6.  \n    - N6 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately. So if we go N9 → N8 → N4 → N6, opponent loses.  \n    - N1 → N5 or N3; N5 ends with no outgoing edges, so opponent loses.  \n    - N3 → N0, which has no outgoing edges → opponent loses.  \n  So from N8, opponent can be forced to a terminal node.  \n- Moving to N2: next player is at N2 → edges to N5 or N7.  \n  - N5 and N7 both have no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N2 causes opponent to lose immediately. This is a winning move.  \nTherefore, choosing N2 maximizes the outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N4, N1\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN9 -> N8, N2\nN4 -> N1, N6\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN7: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N5, N3\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N5, N7\nN3 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N8, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N2.  \n- Moving to N8: next player is at N8, which has edges to N4 and N1.  \n  - From N4: edges to N1 and N6 (N6 is terminal).  \n  - From N1: edges to N5 and N3 (N5 is terminal, N3 to N0 terminal).  \n  - All paths eventually lead to terminal nodes (no outgoing edges), so the player who reaches a terminal node after the opponent's move loses.  \n  - If we go to N8, opponent can go to N4 → N6 (opponent loses). Or N1 → N5 (opponent loses). So opponent can force a win if they play optimally.  \n- Moving to N2: next player is at N2, which has edges to N5 and N7.  \n  - N5 and N7 are both terminal (no outgoing edges). So opponent has no legal move and **loses immediately**.  \nThus, moving to N2 forces the opponent into a position with no outgoing edges, so they lose. This is a winning move.</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N2.  \n- Moving to N8: next player is at N8, which has edges to N4 and N1.  \n  - From N4: edges to N1 and N6 (N6 is terminal).  \n  - From N1: edges to N5 and N3 (N5 is terminal, N3 to N0 terminal).  \n  - All paths eventually lead to terminal nodes (no outgoing edges), so the player who reaches a terminal node after the opponent's move loses.  \n  - If we go to N8, opponent can go to N4 → N6 (opponent loses). Or N1 → N5 (opponent loses). So opponent can force a win if they play optimally.  \n- Moving to N2: next player is at N2, which has edges to N5 and N7.  \n  - N5 and N7 are both terminal (no outgoing edges). So opponent has no legal move and **loses immediately**.  \nThus, moving to N2 forces the opponent into a position with no outgoing edges, so they lose. This is a winning move.</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N4, N1\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN9 -> N8, N2\nN4 -> N1, N6\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN7: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N5, N3\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N5, N7\nN3 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N8, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N2.  \n- Moving to N8: then opponent is at N8, which has edges to N4 and N1.  \n  - If opponent goes to N4, then we are at N4, which leads to N1 or N6.  \n    - N6 is a terminal node (opponent loses if they reach it), so if we go to N4 → N6, opponent loses.  \n    - But opponent can go to N1 → N5 or N3. N5 is terminal, N3 → N0 (terminal). So opponent can force a win from N1.  \n  - If opponent goes to N1, then we are at N1 → N5 (terminal) or N3 → N0 (terminal). So we can force a win.  \n  - So from N8, opponent can choose a path that avoids terminal nodes and forces us into a losing position.  \n- Moving to N2: opponent is at N2 → N5 (terminal) or N7 (terminal). Both are terminal, so opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N2 causes opponent to lose immediately. This is a winning move.</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N2.  \n- Moving to N8: then opponent is at N8, which has edges to N4 and N1.  \n  - If opponent goes to N4, then we are at N4, which leads to N1 or N6.  \n    - N6 is a terminal node (opponent loses if they reach it), so if we go to N4 → N6, opponent loses.  \n    - But opponent can go to N1 → N5 or N3. N5 is terminal, N3 → N0 (terminal). So opponent can force a win from N1.  \n  - If opponent goes to N1, then we are at N1 → N5 (terminal) or N3 → N0 (terminal). So we can force a win.  \n  - So from N8, opponent can choose a path that avoids terminal nodes and forces us into a losing position.  \n- Moving to N2: opponent is at N2 → N5 (terminal) or N7 (terminal). Both are terminal, so opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N2 causes opponent to lose immediately. This is a winning move.</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N4, N1\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN9 -> N8, N2\nN4 -> N1, N6\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN7: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N5, N3\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N5, N7\nN3 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N8, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N2.  \n- Moving to N8: next player is at N8, which has moves to N4 or N1.  \n  - If opponent goes to N4: then to N1 or N6. N6 is terminal (opponent loses). So opponent can go to N1 → N5 (terminal) or N3 → N0 (terminal). Both lead to opponent losing. So N4 leads to opponent losing.  \n  - If opponent goes to N1: leads to N5 (terminal) or N3 → N0 (terminal). Both terminal. So opponent loses.  \n  Thus, from N8, opponent has no safe move — they lose.  \n- Moving to N2: next player goes to N5 (terminal) or N7 (terminal). Both are terminal. So opponent loses immediately.  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing. But we must choose one that maximizes our outcome. Since both result in opponent losing, either is valid. However, moving to N2 leads to immediate terminal outcome for opponent. Moving to N8 gives opponent a choice but still loses. Either is winning. But N2 is simpler and guarantees loss immediately. Choose N2 to ensure immediate win.</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N2.  \n- Moving to N8: next player is at N8, which has moves to N4 or N1.  \n  - If opponent goes to N4: then to N1 or N6. N6 is terminal (opponent loses). So opponent can go to N1 → N5 (terminal) or N3 → N0 (terminal). Both lead to opponent losing. So N4 leads to opponent losing.  \n  - If opponent goes to N1: leads to N5 (terminal) or N3 → N0 (terminal). Both terminal. So opponent loses.  \n  Thus, from N8, opponent has no safe move — they lose.  \n- Moving to N2: next player goes to N5 (terminal) or N7 (terminal). Both are terminal. So opponent loses immediately.  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing. But we must choose one that maximizes our outcome. Since both result in opponent losing, either is valid. However, moving to N2 leads to immediate terminal outcome for opponent. Moving to N8 gives opponent a choice but still loses. Either is winning. But N2 is simpler and guarantees loss immediately. Choose N2 to ensure immediate win.</reason>  \n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N4, N1\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN9 -> N8, N2\nN4 -> N1, N6\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN7: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N5, N3\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N5, N7\nN3 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N8, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N2. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N8: Then opponent is at N8, which has edges to N4 and N1.\n  - If opponent moves to N4: then we are at N4, which has edges to N1 and N6.\n    - If we move to N6 (dead end), opponent loses → we win.\n    - If we move to N1: opponent can go to N5 (dead end) or N3.\n      - If to N3 → opponent moves to N0 (dead end) → opponent loses → we win.\n  - If opponent moves to N1: then we are at N1, which has edges to N5 (dead end) or N3.\n    - If we go to N3 → opponent to N0 → opponent loses → we win.\n  - So from N8, opponent is forced into a position where we can always win.\n\n- Move to N2: opponent at N2 → edges to N5 (dead end) or N7 (dead end).\n  - Both are dead ends → opponent loses immediately → we win.\n\nBoth moves lead to a win, but moving to N2 immediately ends the game with opponent losing on their turn. This is optimal.\n\nThus, choosing N2 maximizes immediate win outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N2. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N8: Then opponent is at N8, which has edges to N4 and N1.\n  - If opponent moves to N4: then we are at N4, which has edges to N1 and N6.\n    - If we move to N6 (dead end), opponent loses → we win.\n    - If we move to N1: opponent can go to N5 (dead end) or N3.\n      - If to N3 → opponent moves to N0 (dead end) → opponent loses → we win.\n  - If opponent moves to N1: then we are at N1, which has edges to N5 (dead end) or N3.\n    - If we go to N3 → opponent to N0 → opponent loses → we win.\n  - So from N8, opponent is forced into a position where we can always win.\n\n- Move to N2: opponent at N2 → edges to N5 (dead end) or N7 (dead end).\n  - Both are dead ends → opponent loses immediately → we win.\n\nBoth moves lead to a win, but moving to N2 immediately ends the game with opponent losing on their turn. This is optimal.\n\nThus, choosing N2 maximizes immediate win outcome.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N9\n\nDirected graph:\nN8 -> N4, N1\nN0: no outgoing edges\nN9 -> N8, N2\nN4 -> N1, N6\nN6: no outgoing edges\nN7: no outgoing edges\nN1 -> N5, N3\nN5: no outgoing edges\nN2 -> N5, N7\nN3 -> N0\n\nLegal moves: N8, N2assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N9, the legal moves are to N8 or N2.  \n- Moving to N8: then opponent is at N8, which has moves to N4 or N1.  \n  - If opponent goes to N4, then we are at N4, which has moves to N1 or N6.  \n    - If we go to N6 (dead end), opponent loses.  \n    - If we go to N1, opponent can go to N5 (dead end) or N3.  \n      - If opponent goes to N3, then we go to N0 (dead end), opponent loses.  \n      - So N4 leads to a winning position.  \n  - If opponent goes to N1 from N8, then we are at N1, which has moves to N5 (dead end) or N3.  \n    - Going to N3 leads to N0 (opponent loses).  \n    - So N1 also leads to a winning position.  \n  Thus, from N8, opponent can only move to N4 or N1, both of which allow us to eventually force a win.  \n- Moving to N2: opponent goes to N5 (dead end) or N7 (dead end). Both are terminal — opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N2 wins immediately. This is better than N8, where opponent gets to move first and can avoid immediate loss.  \nTherefore, the best move is to