[2026-08-18 14:04:47] [agentic_pipeline.py (561)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1863312] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N0\nN2 -> N9, N7\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N2, N7\nN4 -> N3, N1\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N6, N4\nN7 -> N5, N0\nN0: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N6, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, legal moves are to N6 or N4.  \n- Moving to N6: next player is at N2, which has edges to N9 and N7.  \n  - If opponent goes to N9 (dead end), they lose.  \n  - If opponent goes to N7, then we go to N5 or N0 (both dead ends), so we lose.  \n  - So opponent can choose N9 and win.  \n- Moving to N4: next player is at N3 (dead end), so opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N4 forces opponent to face a dead end and wins the game. This is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, legal moves are to N6 or N4.  \n- Moving to N6: next player is at N2, which has edges to N9 and N7.  \n  - If opponent goes to N9 (dead end), they lose.  \n  - If opponent goes to N7, then we go to N5 or N0 (both dead ends), so we lose.  \n  - So opponent can choose N9 and win.  \n- Moving to N4: next player is at N3 (dead end), so opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N4 forces opponent to face a dead end and wins the game. This is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N0\nN2 -> N9, N7\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N2, N7\nN4 -> N3, N1\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N6, N4\nN7 -> N5, N0\nN0: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N6, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, legal moves are to N6 or N4.  \n- Moving to N6: next player is at N2, which has edges to N9 and N7.  \n  - If opponent goes to N9 (dead end), they lose.  \n  - If opponent goes to N7, then we go to N5 or N0 (both dead ends), so we lose.  \n  - So opponent can choose N9 and win.  \n- Moving to N4: next player is at N3 (dead end), so opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N4 forces opponent to face a dead end and wins the game. This is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, legal moves are to N6 or N4.  \n- Moving to N6: next player is at N2, which has edges to N9 and N7.  \n  - If opponent goes to N9 (dead end), they lose.  \n  - If opponent goes to N7, then we go to N5 or N0 (both dead ends), so we lose.  \n  - So opponent can choose N9 and win.  \n- Moving to N4: next player is at N3 (dead end), so opponent loses immediately.  \nThus, moving to N4 forces opponent to face a dead end and wins the game. This is the optimal move.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N0\nN2 -> N9, N7\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N2, N7\nN4 -> N3, N1\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N6, N4\nN7 -> N5, N0\nN0: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N6, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, legal moves are to N6 or N4.  \n- Moving to N6: next player is at N2, which has outgoing edges to N9 and N7.  \n  - If opponent goes to N9 (dead end), they lose.  \n  - If opponent goes to N7, then we are at N5 → N0 (dead end), opponent loses.  \n  So from N2, opponent can only move to N9 or N7, both of which eventually lead to a dead end.  \n  Thus, from N6, opponent is forced into a losing position.  \n- Moving to N4: next player is at N3 (dead end), so opponent loses immediately.  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing, but moving to N4 causes immediate loss for opponent. This is better.  \nThus, choosing N4 maximizes outcome.</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, legal moves are to N6 or N4.  \n- Moving to N6: next player is at N2, which has outgoing edges to N9 and N7.  \n  - If opponent goes to N9 (dead end), they lose.  \n  - If opponent goes to N7, then we are at N5 → N0 (dead end), opponent loses.  \n  So from N2, opponent can only move to N9 or N7, both of which eventually lead to a dead end.  \n  Thus, from N6, opponent is forced into a losing position.  \n- Moving to N4: next player is at N3 (dead end), so opponent loses immediately.  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing, but moving to N4 causes immediate loss for opponent. This is better.  \nThus, choosing N4 maximizes outcome.</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N0\nN2 -> N9, N7\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N2, N7\nN4 -> N3, N1\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N6, N4\nN7 -> N5, N0\nN0: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N6, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, legal moves are to N6 or N4.  \n- Moving to N6: next player is at N2, which has outgoing edges to N9 and N7.  \n  - If opponent goes to N9 (dead end), they lose.  \n  - If opponent goes to N7, then we are at N5 → N0 (dead end), opponent loses.  \n  So from N2, opponent can only move to N9 or N7, both of which lead to a dead end or back to N5 → N0. Either way, opponent eventually loses.  \n- Moving to N4: next player is at N3, which has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately.  \n\nThus, moving to N4 causes immediate opponent loss. This is better than N6, where opponent has choices. Therefore, N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, legal moves are to N6 or N4.  \n- Moving to N6: next player is at N2, which has outgoing edges to N9 and N7.  \n  - If opponent goes to N9 (dead end), they lose.  \n  - If opponent goes to N7, then we are at N5 → N0 (dead end), opponent loses.  \n  So from N2, opponent can only move to N9 or N7, both of which lead to a dead end or back to N5 → N0. Either way, opponent eventually loses.  \n- Moving to N4: next player is at N3, which has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately.  \n\nThus, moving to N4 causes immediate opponent loss. This is better than N6, where opponent has choices. Therefore, N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N0\nN2 -> N9, N7\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N2, N7\nN4 -> N3, N1\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N6, N4\nN7 -> N5, N0\nN0: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N6, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, legal moves are to N6 or N4.  \n- Moving to N6: next player is at N2, which has edges to N9 and N7.  \n  - If opponent goes to N9 (dead end), they lose.  \n  - If opponent goes to N7, then we are at N5 → N0 (dead end), opponent loses.  \n  So from N2, opponent can only move to N9 or N7, both of which lead to a dead end eventually.  \n  Thus, from N6, opponent is forced into a losing position.  \n- Moving to N4: next player is at N3 (dead end), so opponent loses immediately.  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing, but moving to N4 causes immediate loss for opponent. This is better.  \nThus, N4 is optimal because it results in opponent losing on their first move.</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, legal moves are to N6 or N4.  \n- Moving to N6: next player is at N2, which has edges to N9 and N7.  \n  - If opponent goes to N9 (dead end), they lose.  \n  - If opponent goes to N7, then we are at N5 → N0 (dead end), opponent loses.  \n  So from N2, opponent can only move to N9 or N7, both of which lead to a dead end eventually.  \n  Thus, from N6, opponent is forced into a losing position.  \n- Moving to N4: next player is at N3 (dead end), so opponent loses immediately.  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing, but moving to N4 causes immediate loss for opponent. This is better.  \nThus, N4 is optimal because it results in opponent losing on their first move.</reason>  \n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N0\nN2 -> N9, N7\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N2, N7\nN4 -> N3, N1\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N6, N4\nN7 -> N5, N0\nN0: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N6, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N6 or N4. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N6: Then opponent is at N6, which has edges to N2 and N7.\n  - If opponent goes to N2: then we are at N2, which has edges to N9 and N7.\n    - If we go to N9 (dead end), opponent loses.\n    - If we go to N7: opponent moves to N5 or N0 (both eventually lead to dead ends).\n    - N7 → N5 → N0 (dead end) or N7 → N0 (dead end). So opponent loses.\n  - If opponent goes to N7: then we are at N7 → N5 or N0 (dead end). Opponent loses.\n\n- Move to N4: Then opponent is at N4, which has edges to N3 and N1 (both dead ends). So opponent loses immediately.\n\nThus, moving to N4 causes the opponent to face a dead end immediately. This is a winning move.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N6 or N4. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N6: Then opponent is at N6, which has edges to N2 and N7.\n  - If opponent goes to N2: then we are at N2, which has edges to N9 and N7.\n    - If we go to N9 (dead end), opponent loses.\n    - If we go to N7: opponent moves to N5 or N0 (both eventually lead to dead ends).\n    - N7 → N5 → N0 (dead end) or N7 → N0 (dead end). So opponent loses.\n  - If opponent goes to N7: then we are at N7 → N5 or N0 (dead end). Opponent loses.\n\n- Move to N4: Then opponent is at N4, which has edges to N3 and N1 (both dead ends). So opponent loses immediately.\n\nThus, moving to N4 causes the opponent to face a dead end immediately. This is a winning move.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N0\nN2 -> N9, N7\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N2, N7\nN4 -> N3, N1\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N6, N4\nN7 -> N5, N0\nN0: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N6, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N6 or N4. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N6: Then opponent is at N6, which has edges to N2 and N7.\n  - If opponent goes to N2: then we are at N2, which has edges to N9 and N7.\n    - If we go to N9 (dead end), opponent loses → we win.\n    - If we go to N7: opponent moves to N5 or N0 (both eventually lead to dead ends).\n      - N5 → N0 (dead end), so opponent loses.\n  - If opponent goes to N7: then we are at N7, which has edges to N5 and N0 (both dead ends), so opponent loses.\n\nThus, from N6, no matter what opponent does, we can force a win.\n\n- Move to N4: Then opponent is at N4, which has edges to N3 and N1 (both dead ends). So opponent loses immediately.\n\nThus, both moves win, but moving to N4 causes opponent to lose immediately — a stronger outcome.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N6 or N4. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N6: Then opponent is at N6, which has edges to N2 and N7.\n  - If opponent goes to N2: then we are at N2, which has edges to N9 and N7.\n    - If we go to N9 (dead end), opponent loses → we win.\n    - If we go to N7: opponent moves to N5 or N0 (both eventually lead to dead ends).\n      - N5 → N0 (dead end), so opponent loses.\n  - If opponent goes to N7: then we are at N7, which has edges to N5 and N0 (both dead ends), so opponent loses.\n\nThus, from N6, no matter what opponent does, we can force a win.\n\n- Move to N4: Then opponent is at N4, which has edges to N3 and N1 (both dead ends). So opponent loses immediately.\n\nThus, both moves win, but moving to N4 causes opponent to lose immediately — a stronger outcome.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to N4.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N8\n\nDirected graph:\nN5 -> N0\nN2 -> N9, N7\nN3: no outgoing edges\nN9: no outgoing edges\nN6 -> N2, N7\nN4 -> N3, N1\nN1: no outgoing edges\nN8 -> N6, N4\nN7 -> N5, N0\nN0: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N6, N4assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N6 or N4. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N6: Then opponent is at N6, which has edges to N2 and N7.\n  - If opponent goes to N2: then we are at N2, which has edges to N9 and N7.\n    - If we go to N9 (dead end), opponent loses → we win.\n    - If we go to N7: opponent at N7 → to N5 or N0 (both eventually lead to dead ends).\n      - N5 → N0 (dead end), opponent loses.\n      - So from N2, we can force a win.\n  - If opponent goes to N7: then we are at N7 → to N5 or N0 (both dead ends), opponent loses.\n  - So from N6, opponent is forced into a position where they eventually lose.\n\n- Move to N4: Then opponent is at N4, which has edges to N3 and N1 (both dead ends).\n  - Opponent must move to N3 or N1 → both are dead ends → opponent loses immediately.\n  - So this is a winning move too.\n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing, but we want to maximize outcome. Since both are winning, we choose the one that gives us more control or is safer. N4 leads to immediate loss for opponent (no choice), so it is stronger and simpler.\n\nThus, choosing N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Starting at N8, the legal moves are to N6 or N4. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N6: Then opponent is at N6, which has edges to N2 and N7.\n  - If opponent goes to N2: then we are at N2, which has edges to N9 and N7.\n    - If we go to N9 (dead end), opponent loses → we win.\n    - If we go to N7: opponent at N7 → to N5 or N0 (both eventually lead to dead ends).\n      - N5 → N0 (dead end), opponent loses.\n      - So from N2, we can force a win.\n  - If opponent goes to N7: then we are at N7 → to N5 or N0 (both dead ends), opponent loses.\n  - So from N6, opponent is forced into a position where they eventually lose.\n\n- Move to N4: Then opponent is at N4, which has edges to N3 and N1 (both dead ends).\n  - Opponent must move to N3 or N1 → both are dead ends → opponent loses immediately.\n  - So this is a winning move too.\n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing, but we want to maximize outcome. Since both are winning, we choose the one that gives us more control or is safer. N4 leads to immediate loss for opponent (no choice), so it is stronger and simpler.\n\nThus, choosing N4 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N4</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N1\n\nDirected graph:\nN10: no outgoing edges\nN12 -> N9, N4\nN11: no outgoing edges\nN0 -> N11, N10\nN9 -> N6, N5\nN6: no outgoing e