[2026-08-18 14:06:34] [agentic_pipeline.py (561)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 1863312] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N4, N0\nN7: no outgoing edges\nN0 -> N5, N7\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN5 -> N2\nN9 -> N8, N7\nN3 -> N1\nN6 -> N3, N9, N0\nN2: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N3, N9, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Current node is N6. From N6, legal moves are to N3, N9, or N0. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a node with no outgoing edges or a losing position).  \n- Move to N3: N3 → N1 → N4 or N0. N4 has no outgoing edges, so if opponent reaches N4, they lose. But N1 → N0, and N0 → N5 → N2 (N2 has no outgoing edges), so opponent can reach N2 and lose. So from N3, opponent can eventually lose.  \n- Move to N9: N9 → N8 or N7. Both N8 and N7 have no outgoing edges. So opponent immediately loses. This is a winning move.  \n- Move to N0: N0 → N5 → N2 (N2 has no outgoing edges), so opponent reaches N2 and loses. Also valid, but N9 gives immediate loss to opponent.  \n\nN9 leads to immediate loss for opponent (both N8 and N7 are terminal). Thus, N9 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Current node is N6. From N6, legal moves are to N3, N9, or N0. We analyze each option to determine which leads to a winning position (i.e., forces the opponent into a node with no outgoing edges or a losing position).  \n- Move to N3: N3 → N1 → N4 or N0. N4 has no outgoing edges, so if opponent reaches N4, they lose. But N1 → N0, and N0 → N5 → N2 (N2 has no outgoing edges), so opponent can reach N2 and lose. So from N3, opponent can eventually lose.  \n- Move to N9: N9 → N8 or N7. Both N8 and N7 have no outgoing edges. So opponent immediately loses. This is a winning move.  \n- Move to N0: N0 → N5 → N2 (N2 has no outgoing edges), so opponent reaches N2 and loses. Also valid, but N9 gives immediate loss to opponent.  \n\nN9 leads to immediate loss for opponent (both N8 and N7 are terminal). Thus, N9 is optimal.  \n</reason>\n<answer>N9</answer>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are playing Directed Acyclic Graph Vertex Geography.user\nThe players alternate moving a token along one directed edge. After you move to a destination, the opponent acts from that destination. If that destination has no outgoing edges, the opponent loses. A player with no legal move loses. Choose one legal destination that maximizes your final game outcome. Keep your thinking process concise.\n\nOutput exactly:\n<reason>your analysis</reason>\n<answer>NODE</answer>\n\nDo not output anything else. A response that does not follow this format results in immediate loss.\n\nCurrent node: N6\n\nDirected graph:\nN1 -> N4, N0\nN7: no outgoing edges\nN0 -> N5, N7\nN4: no outgoing edges\nN8: no outgoing edges\nN5 -> N2\nN9 -> N8, N7\nN3 -> N1\nN6 -> N3, N9, N0\nN2: no outgoing edges\n\nLegal moves: N3, N9, N0assistant\n", "response": "<reason>Current node is N6. From N6, legal moves are to N3, N9, or N0. We analyze each:\n\n- Move to N3: goes to N1. From N1, moves to N4 or N0. \n  - N4 has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately → we win.\n  - N0 → N5 → N2 (N2 has no outgoing edges) → opponent loses → we win.\n  So from N1, opponent loses in both cases. Thus, moving to N3 wins.\n\n- Move to N9: goes to N8 or N7. \n  - N8 and N7 have no 