ng to N3: next node is N6, which leads to N9 or N12.  \n  - N9 → N8 (no outgoing edges) or N0 → N11 → N8 (opponent loses).  \n  - N12 → N13 → N10 → N4 (no outgoing edges) → opponent loses.  \n  So from N3, opponent eventually loses too.  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing, but we must choose one that maximizes outcome. Since both result in opponent losing, either is valid. However, moving to N2 leads directly to N7 (no outgoing edges), ending the game immediately. This is simpler and guarantees win. Thus, N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>", "episode_score": -0.95, "llm_raw_text": "<reason>Current node is N5. From N5, legal moves are to N2 or N3.  \n- Moving to N2: next node is N7, which has no outgoing edges → opponent loses immediately. So this is a winning move.  \n- Moving to N3: next node is N6, which leads to N9 or N12.  \n  - N9 → N8 (no outgoing edges) or N0 → N11 → N8 (opponent loses).  \n  - N12 → N13 → N10 → N4 (no outgoing edges) → opponent loses.  \n  So from N3, opponent eventually loses too.  \n\nBoth moves lead to opponent losing, but we must choose one that maximizes outcome. Since both result in opponent losing, either is valid. However, moving to N2 leads directly to N7 (no outgoing edges), ending the game immediately. This is simpler and guarantees win. Thus, N2 is optimal.</reason>\n<answer>N2</answer>"}]
